        "content": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"As Compliance Officer, I view Article 12 not as a bureaucratic checkbox but as an architectural mandate that constrains every system we ship into the EU market. The 'shall technically allow' language in paragraph 1 is non-negotiable: our engineering teams must embed automatic event logging into the core runtime of each high-risk system before any deployment, and for Annex III point 1(a) biometric identification tools we face four specific data-capture obligations that cannot be retrofitted after launch. Recital 71 compounds this by requiring that technical documentation remain current across the entire product lifetime, meaning our R&D processes must include a standing documentation-maintenance function rather than treating it as a one-time pre-market deliverable. Failure here is not merely a fine risk; under the Act's enforcement regime, inability to demonstrate traceability effectively bars us from placing systems on the EU market at all, which for an institution whose revenue depends on that access is an existential threat.\",\n  \"stance\": 62,\n  \"proposals\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Runtime Logging Infrastructure as a Core Component\",\n      \"what\": \"A cross-team engineering workstream that designs and integrates an automatic event-logging pipeline into the runtime architecture of every high-risk AI system we develop, ensuring continuous capture of risk-relevant events over the full operational lifetime.\",\n      \"how\": \"We would task a dedicated platform sub-group to build a shared logging SDK with configurable schemas for start and end timestamps, risk-trigger flags, and post-market monitoring hooks, then mandate its inclusion in our internal design-review checklist before any system enters integration testing.\",\n      \"why\": \"This converts an open-ended regulatory obligation into a bounded engineering deliverable, protecting the lab from market-access disqualification while reducing per-project compliance costs through platform reuse.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Living Technical Documentation Lifecycle Programme\",\n      \"what\": \"A standing operational process that treats the technical documentation required by Recital 71 as a continuously maintained artefact rather than a static pre-market filing, covering system characteristics, algorithm versions, training-data lineage, and risk-management changes across the product's full life.\",\n      \"how\": \"We would assign each deployed high-risk system to a named documentation steward who triggers a revision cycle at every substantial modification, model update, or incident report, using version-controlled repositories with audit trails accessible to notified bodies upon request.\",\n      \"why\": \"This insulates the institution against the reputational and legal exposure of presenting outdated documentation during a market-surveillance inspection, directly supporting our survival in regulated EU markets.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Annex III Biometric-ID Verification and Match-Trace Module\",\n      \"what\": \"A product-engineering sprint to build the four data-capture functions mandated by Article 12(3) for our biometric identification tools—use-period timestamps, reference-database identifiers, matched input-data records, and verifier identity logs—into a dedicated compliance module.\",\n      \"how\": \"We would stand up a cross-functional pod of two engineers, one data-protection specialist, and one domain researcher to prototype the module against our existing biometric pipeline within a quarter, then validate it with external EU legal counsel before production rollout.\",\n      \"why\": \"Without this module our biometric-ID systems cannot lawfully operate in the EU, so building it now prevents a forced withdrawal that would damage both revenue and institutional reputation.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
