ity to operate in EU\n- Regulatory compliance: The logging is a hard \"shall\" requirement - no discretion\n- Market reputation: Being seen as compliant builds trust; being caught non-compliant destroys it\n\nFor the R&D agenda change: This isn't about changing WHAT we build (the AI systems), but HOW we build them. The logging infrastructure needs to be embedded in the architecture. For Annex III 1(a) systems, there are very specific data points required. This is significant but not a complete overhaul - it's more of an architectural requirement that affects how systems are designed and maintained. I'd say around 55-65 range. It's a moderate-to-significant adjustment because logging infrastructure needs to be built into the core architecture, documentation processes need overhauling, and for biometric ID systems specifically there are very concrete data requirements. But it doesn't change what problems we solve - it changes how we build and maintain solutions. I'll go with 62.\n\nNow for 3 distinct proposals:\n\n1. Something about engineering/architecture - embedding logging infrastructure into the system architecture itself. This is a technical R&D change.\n2. Something about documentation lifecycle management - Recital 71 emphasizes keeping docs up to date throughout lifetime. This is an operational/process change.\n3. Something specific to Annex III 1(a) compliance - building the specific verification and database-tracking modules required by Article 12(3).\n\nLet me make sure these are genuinely different:\n- Proposal 1: Engineering the logging pipeline (technical architecture)\n- Proposal 2: Documentation lifecycle system (process/operational)\n- Proposal 3: Building a compliance-specific module for biometric ID systems (product feature / regulatory engineering)\n\nNow let me write each with title, what, how, why - keeping total words between 60-110 per proposal.\n\nLet me draft:\n\n**Reasoning (3-5 sentences):**\nAs Compliance Officer, I view Article 12 not as