ire architectural changes and process adjustments. However, if the lab is currently not building logging infrastructure, this could be significant. But it's more of an implementation requirement than a strategic pivot. I'd say around 40-55. Let me think... For a compliance officer concerned with institutional survival, this is important but doesn't require overhauling what the lab works on. It requires changing HOW things are built and operated, not WHAT is built. Maybe 45. Actually, if we consider that logging requirements could affect architecture decisions early in development, it's moderate. I'll go with 45.\n\nNow for 3 distinct proposals:\n\n1. Something about integrating a logging framework into the R&D pipeline (technical/architectural)\n2. Something about post-market monitoring infrastructure and processes (operational/organizational)\n3. Something about training/documentation and compliance verification (governance/training)\n\nLet me make them genuinely different:\n- Proposal 1: Technical - build a standardized logging middleware/framework into all system architectures\n- Proposal 2: Operational - create a post-market monitoring unit with dashboards for log analysis\n- Proposal 3: Governance/Strategic - embed compliance checkpoints in the R&D lifecycle (like a \"logging gate\" before systems go to testing)\n\nWait, let me reconsider to make them more distinct:\n- Proposal 1: Technical architecture change - integrate automatic event logging as a core module in every high-risk system design\n- Proposal 2: Organizational/process - establish a dedicated post-market surveillance team that reviews logs continuously\n- Proposal 3: Research/development - develop open-source or internal tooling for automated compliance verification of log completeness\n\nLet me now write each proposal and count words carefully (60-110 words across all four fields).\n\nProposal 1:\nTitle: \"Mandatory Logging Module in System Architecture\" \nWhat: Embed a standardized, tamper-evident event-logging middleware into every high-risk AI system's reference architecture so that start/end timestamps, input data references, and risk-trigger events are captured automatically from day one. (29 words)\nHow: The platform engineering team will define a common logging API spec aligned to Article 12(3), integrate it as a non-negotiable dependency in our internal framework, and enforce its presence through CI/CD gates before any system exits the lab. (36 words)\nWhy: Without this technical capability we cannot lawfully place high-risk systems on the EU market, making every product line non-compliant by default. (19 words)\n\nTotal: 8 + 29 + 36 + 19 = 92 words. Good, within 60-110.\n\nProposal 2:\nTitle: \"Standing Post-Market Log Surveillance Cell\" \nWhat: Create a small permanent unit of compliance analysts who continuously review system logs for risk indicators, substantial modifications, and anomalies flagged under Articles 72 and 79(1). (24 words)\nHow: The cell will operate from a dedicated monitoring dashboard fed by the logging middleware, run weekly exception reports, and maintain an escalation protocol that triggers engineering review within defined timeframes. (30 words)\nWhy: Continuous operational oversight protects our market reputation by catching drift or misuse before regulators or affected persons report it to us first. (21 words)\n\nTotal: 7 + 24 + 30 + 21 = 82 words. Good.\n\nProposal 3:\nTitle: \"Log-Completeness Audit and Verification Protocol\" \nWhat: Develop an internal verification protocol that systematically checks whether each deployed system's logs satisfy the minimum fields required by Article 12(3), including reference database identity, matched input data, and verifier identification for biometric use-cases. (35 words)\nHow: A quarterly audit team will sample production logs against a compliance checklist, document gaps in a register, and report findings to the board with remediation deadlines tied to the next release cycle. (