lection.\",\n      \"why\": \"This converts an obligatory compliance cost into a unique scientific asset, giving us first-mover publication advantage on long-horizon model reliability questions no voluntary study could address at this scale.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Open Validation Documentation Standard for the Field\",\n      \"what\": \"We develop an open technical documentation template satisfying Recital 71's requirements for clear records of algorithms, data, training, testing, and validation processes, designed for adoption by any research lab.\",\n      \"how\": \"A cross-project working group drafts the standard during our mandatory documentation cycles, then publishes it under an academic license with a companion paper arguing structured reproducibility documentation is itself a methodological advance in AI science.\",\n      \"why\": \"This elevates a compliance obligation into a field-level contribution, positions the institution as a thought leader on responsible-AI methodology, and draws external collaborators who need the template.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Log-Native Protocols for R&D Experiment Design\",\n      \"what\": \"We develop log-native experimental protocols in which Article 12's logging architecture is embedded from the first integration build, so every research prototype generates compliant telemetry at inception rather than as a retrofitted afterthought.\",\n      \"how\": \"The methodology team writes an internal protocol handbook specifying how to structure model-iteration cycles, A/B phases, and ablation studies so that timestamps, reference-database references, and verification events are first-class experimental variables.\",\n      \"why\": \"This protects intellectual merit by anchoring published results on already-audit-ready data and contributes a reproducible experimental framework the broader field can adopt.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
