y retrofitting and lets us demonstrate compliance to notified bodies without scramble. (18 words)\n\nTotal: 7+26+30+18 = 81 words. Good, within 60-110.\n\nProposal 2:\n- title: Lifetime Log Retention and Monitoring Pipeline (7 words)\n- what: An operational infrastructure that stores, indexes, and makes retrievable all system logs for the full lifetime of each deployed high-risk AI system, aligned with Article 72 post-market monitoring duties. (30 words)\n- how: We will designate a data-stewardship team to define retention schedules, access controls, and automated integrity checks on stored logs, reviewed quarterly by Compliance and reported to management. (26 words)\n- why: A functioning log pipeline is the evidentiary backbone that proves we monitored operations throughout the system's life, protecting both our license to operate and market trust. (27 words)\n\nTotal: 7+30+26+27 = 90 words. Good.\n\nProposal 3:\n- title: Annex III Biometric Logging Compliance Audit Protocol (8 words)\n- what: A targeted verification procedure confirming that our biometric-identification systems meet the four minimum logging fields in Article 12(3): use-period timestamps, reference-database identifiers, matched input data, and verifying-person identification. (29 words)\n- how: Legal and engineering staff will co-author a checklist-based audit, run each quarter against every active Annex III point 1(a) deployment, with findings escalated to the board if gaps appear. (28 words)\n- why: These are the fields most likely to draw regulator scrutiny first; demonstrating them proactively shields our reputation and preempts enforcement action. (20 words)\n\nTotal: 8+29+28+20 = 85 words. Good.\n\nNow let me write the 3-5 sentence reasoning paragraph from my Compliance Officer perspective:\n\nAs a Compliance Officer, I see Article 12 not as a documentation checkbox but as an engineering and operational mandate that is embedded in how our systems are designed, built, and run over their entire