n(10) tiers,(11) encryption(12) standards,(13) and(14) audit(15) cadence;(16) a(17) lightweight(18) dashboard(19) surfaces(20) anomalies(21) flagged(22) by(23) the(24) logging(25) layer(26) to(27) the(28) risk-management(29) function(30) under(31) Article(32) 72.(33) = 33 words\nwhy: Regulators(1) will(2) inspect(3) logs(4) years(5) after(6) deployment,(7) so(8) demonstrating(9) continuous(10) stewardship(11) is(12) what(13) separates(14) a(15) compliant(16) institution(17) from(18) one(19) facing(20) suspension(21) of(22) its(23) CE(24) marking.(25) = 25 words\n\nTotal: 6 + 31 + 33 + 25 = 95 words. Good.\n\nNow let me write the reasoning section (3-5 sentences) and pick my number.\n\nReasoning (3-5 sentences):\nAs Compliance Officer, I see Article 12 not as a documentation chore but as an architectural mandate: \"technically allow for automatic recording\" means our engineering teams must design logging into every high-risk system from the first prototype iteration, not treat it as a late-stage compliance checkbox. The lifetime dimension is critical—these logs must survive and remain accessible across years of deployment, which creates ongoing infrastructure costs and data-governance obligations that the lab has never budgeted for at this scale. For our Annex III point 1(a) biometric products, the four minimum log fields are explicit and non-negotiable; failing to capture any one of them is a straightforward regulatory breach with no \"best-efforts\" defence. I also note that Recital 71 ties logging directly to post-market monitoring and risk identification under Articles 79(1) and 72, meaning our logs must be analytically useful, not merely stored—this elevates the requirement from IT plumbing to a data-science concern within R&D itself. Practically, I would direct project leads to treat logging architecture as a first-class design deliverable in every sprint review for the next two quarters.\n\nThat's 5 sentences. Good.\n\nNumber: I'll go with 38. It's a moderate