o shared infrastructure keeps per-project overhead minimal, protects scarce R&D hours for actual science, and avoids duplicated storage spend across parallel grant-funded teams.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Efficient Lifetime Logging as a Research Workstream\",\n      \"what\": \"A funded internal research track investigating compression, selective retention, and queryable indexing of high-volume event logs so that traceability obligations over multi-year system lifetimes do not consume disproportionate storage and compute budgets.\",\n      \"how\": \"We carve out fifteen percent of one research group's capacity for eighteen months, partnering with our systems-engineering team to prototype log-reduction techniques validated against the Article 12(2) traceability thresholds.\",\n      \"why\": \"Turning a compliance cost into a publishable contribution protects the lab's scientific-output metrics while directly reducing the long-term operational expense that would otherwise erode grant margins.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Milestone Gates Tying Logging Readiness to Funding Releases\",\n      \"what\": \"A restructuring of our project-management protocol so that no phase-gate funding release occurs until the logging capability for that development stage has been demonstrated and signed off by a compliance checkpoint.\",\n      \"how\": \"We amend our existing Gantt-based budget-release templates to insert two new verification nodes—one at system-design completion and one before deployment—where a joint engineering-and-compliance review confirms Article 12 conformance.\",\n      \"why\": \"This front-loads the cost of logging into earlier, cheaper development phases rather than expensive late-stage rework, protecting both schedule integrity and the funding envelope each grant provides.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
