ting infrastructure: we likely already have some logging for debugging purposes. The question is whether it meets the Article 12 standard (traceability appropriate to intended purpose)\n\nMy stance should probably be around 40-55 range. It's not a major overhaul of what we work on (the science stays the same), but it does require meaningful changes to how we allocate engineering resources, update our development pipelines, and potentially invest in new infrastructure for long-term logging. It's a moderate adjustment - we don't pivot research directions, but we do need to restructure how engineering hours are allocated and ensure compliance is built into the SDLC rather than tacked on.\n\nLet me settle on 48/100 - it's a real constraint that reshapes resource allocation but doesn't change our fundamental research agenda. The science stays; the engineering pipeline and infrastructure investments shift.\n\nNow for 3 distinct, concrete proposals:\n\n1. **Infrastructure investment**: Build a unified logging-as-a-service layer into our existing R&D infrastructure. This is about leveraging what we already have (servers, storage) rather than building per-project logging solutions. Think of it as a shared service that all high-risk projects plug into. This is an infrastructure/resource-allocation play.\n\n2. **Research pivot on logging efficiency**: Since logging \"over the lifetime\" of systems could be enormously resource-intensive (storage costs, compute for processing), there's actually a research question here: how do you design efficient, compressed, queryable event logs that meet traceability requirements without blowing up storage budgets? This turns a compliance cost into a research opportunity. We can develop novel logging compression/indexing techniques as a side output of our R&D.\n\n3. **Process/SDLC restructuring**: Redefine project milestone gates so that logging capability is a go/no-go criterion at specific development stages (design review, pre-deployment, 