        "content": "{\n\"reasoning\": \"Article 12 does not redirect the scientific questions we pursue—it constrains the engineering substrate—but it opens a scientifically valuable side-channel our lab is uniquely positioned to exploit. Mandated lifetime logging produces operational telemetry at a scale and granularity that no academic dataset can match, giving us a natural laboratory for studying model drift, edge-case failure, and distributional shift in ways that directly feed interpretability and robustness research. From an intellectual-merit standpoint, the documentation requirements in Recital 71 push our group toward reproducibility standards that, if we publish them as open norms, raise the evidentiary bar for the whole field rather than merely satisfying a regulator. The broader scientific impact comes from treating compliance data not as waste heat but as first-class research input: every logged event is a potential probe into why a model did what it did under conditions our training set never covered. Our R&D agenda should absorb this as a methodological upgrade and a modest new workstream, not as a redirect of core scientific focus.\",\n\"stance\": 42,\n\"proposals\": [\n{\n\"title\": \"Deployment Telemetry as Robustness Research Corpus\",\n\"what\": \"A research project that treats mandated operational logs from our deployed high-risk systems as a longitudinal dataset for studying model drift, edge-case failures, and distributional shift beyond training conditions.\",\n\"how\": \"We will build an internal analytics pipeline over the Article 12(3) event streams—timestamps, reference-database hits, input–output pairs, verification events—and run scheduled robustness probes, publishing findings as preprints on post-deployment behaviour.\",\n\"why\": \"This converts a compliance obligation into a scientific asset no non-EU lab can replicate, strengthening our interpretability line and the broader evidence base for AI safety.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Open S