lidation processes\" documentation pushes toward reproducibility standards that benefit the broader field\n   - However, it doesn't fundamentally change WHAT scientific problems we tackle — it changes HOW we build and deploy\n\n3. From intellectual merit:\n   - Good logging practices could feed back into research on model interpretability and robustness\n   - The data collected through mandated logging could become a research resource\n   - But the regulation itself doesn't demand new science — it demands engineering compliance\n\n4. From broader scientific impact:\n   - Our documentation standards (if we exceed minimums) could set field norms\n   - Open-sourcing our logging frameworks or documenting our approaches could benefit the community\n   - The regulatory pressure creates a natural incentive to publish on interpretability and monitoring\n\nMy stance should be moderate. This regulation doesn't change WHAT science we do, but it changes HOW we build systems and gives us new data streams that could inform research. It's not zero (there ARE changes) and it's not 100 (the fundamental scientific questions remain the same). I'd say around 38-45 range — moderate adjustment to methodology and some research opportunity in the logging/monitoring space, but the core agenda stays.\n\nLet me settle on ~42/100. The regulation creates interesting research synergies but doesn't redirect the lab's scientific mission. It's a methodological constraint with some upside for data-rich research.\n\nNow for 3 distinct proposals:\n\n1. Something about leveraging logging as a research resource — building a \"deployment telemetry\" research program that studies model drift, failure modes from real operational logs. This is different from just complying; it's turning the compliance requirement into a scientific asset.\n\n2. Something about documentation/reproducibility standards — developing and publishing open frameworks for AI system technical documentation that go beyond Article 12 minimums, setting field norms. This serves broader scientific impact by making our systems more transparent and reproducible for other researchers.\n\n3. Something about the intersection of logging and interpretability research — specifically, using the mandated event-logging infrastructure as a natural experiment for studying when/why models fail in production, feeding insights back into training methodology research. Wait, this overlaps with #1 too much.\n\nLet me rethink:\n1. Research project: Using operational logs to study model drift and edge-case behavior (turning compliance data into scientific insight about robustness)\n2. Infrastructure/methodology: Developing an open-source logging and documentation framework that exceeds Article 12 minimums, published as a standard for the field (broader impact through setting norms)\n3. Something more fundamental to the research agenda: A pilot project on \"logging-native model design\" — exploring whether designing models with first-class observability/logging in mind changes the architecture or training process in ways that are scientifically interesting and publishable, not just compliance-driven.\n\nThese three are distinct:\n- #1 is a data-science/analytics project using existing logs\n- #2 is an engineering/standards contribution to the field\n- #3 is a fundamental research question about model design philosophy\n\nLet me write these up carefully, keeping each between 60-110 words across all four fields combined.\n\nLet me draft:\n\n(1) Reasoning (3-5 sentences):\nArticle 12 does not redirect the scientific questions we pursue—it constrains the engineering substrate—but it does open a scientifically valuable side-channel that our lab is uniquely positioned to exploit. Mandated lifetime logging produces operational telemetry at scale that no academic dataset can match, giving us a natural laboratory for studying model drift, edge-case failure, and distributional shift in ways that directly feed interpretability and robustness research. From an intellectual-merit standpoint, the documentation requirements in Recital 71 push our group toward reproducibility standards that, if we publish them as open norms, would raise the evidentiary bar for the whole field rather than merely satisfying a regulator. The broader scientific impact comes from treating compliance data not as waste heat but as first-class research input: every logged event is a potential probe into why a model did what it did under conditions our training set never covered. Our R&D agenda should absorb this as a methodological upgrade and a modest new workstream, not as a redirect of core scientific focus.\n\nThat's 5 sentences. Good.\n\nStance: 42/100\n\nNow the proposals:\n\nProposal 1: \"Deployment Telemetry as Robustness Research Corpus\"\n- what: A research project that treats mandated operational logs from our deployed high-risk systems as a longitudinal dataset for studying model drift, edge-case failures, and distributional shift beyond training conditions.\n- how: We will build an internal analytics pipeline over the Article 12(3) event streams (start/end timestamps, reference database hits, input–output pairs, verification events) and run scheduled robustness probes, with findings published as preprints and papers on post-deployment model behavior.\n- why: This converts a compliance obligation into a unique scientific asset that no lab without EU-market deployment can replicate, strengthening both our interpretability research line and the broader evidence base for AI safety.\n\nLet me count words: \ntitle: 6 words (fine, ≤10)\nwhat: \"A research project that treats mandated operational logs from our deployed high-risk systems as a longitudinal dataset for studying model drift, edge-case failures, and distributional shift beyond training conditions.\" = ~32 words\nhow: \"We will build an internal analytics pipeline over the Article 12(3) event streams (start/end timestamps, reference database hits, input–out