ectures, benchmark it across model families on standard high-risk tasks such as document classification and anomaly detection, and publish open-source libraries embedding compliant logging hooks at the tensor-operation level.\",\n      \"why\": \"It converts a compliance obligation into a genuine contribution to interpretability science while giving our engineering teams off-the-shelf components that reduce per-project logging costs.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Anonymised Operational Logs as an Open Corpus\",\n      \"what\": \"Building a governance-approved pipeline that transforms the mandatory Article 12 logs from deployed Annex III systems into de-identified, schema-standardised datasets released under open licences for the broader AI-reliability research community.\",\n      \"how\": \"A small cross-functional team of two data engineers, one ethicist, and one ML researcher would define an anonymisation protocol cleared by our institutional review board, build automated tooling that tags log events with the Article 12(2)(a)-(c) categories, and publish quarterly releases on a European open-science platform.\",\n      \"why\": \"It positions the lab as a steward of new scientific data resources, strengthens public trust in high-risk AI through transparency, and creates citation-drawing assets for our researchers.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Benchmark Suite for Risk-Event Detection in Logs\",\n      \"what\": \"Designing and releasing a public benchmark that operationalises Article 12(2)(a)'s requirement to identify situations where a system 'may present a risk,' reframing it as the formal problem of anomalous-inference detection from streaming operational logs.\",\n      \"how\": \"We would curate synthetic and anonymised real log sequences labelled with known failure modes, define standard metrics for detection latency and false-alarm rate, and host an annual community challenge where external groups compete their detector algorithms against our