e WHAT science we pursue (our research questions remain the same), but it does impose constraints on HOW we build systems that could influence architectural choices (favoring interpretability, modularity). It's a moderate adjustment - we don't pivot our agenda, but we do need to integrate traceability as a design principle that might actually open interesting research sub-questions.\n\nLet me think about 3 distinct proposals:\n\n1. A research project on \"traceable inference\" or interpretable logging - turning the compliance requirement into a research opportunity. Study how different model architectures (transformers, GNNs, ensemble methods) naturally lend themselves to event-level traceability vs. black-box opacity. This is genuinely a knowledge-frontier question: can we design models whose internal decision path IS the log?\n\n2. A data-science / open-science project: anonymized operational logs from deployed high-risk systems as a new class of research dataset. The Act requires us to keep these logs; we could (with proper anonymization and ethical review) develop methods for extracting scientific insights about model behavior, drift, edge cases from aggregate log data. This advances the field's understanding of AI in-the-wild.\n\n3. A benchmarking/evaluation framework project: Article 12(2)(a) requires identifying \"situations that may result in... presenting a risk\" - this is essentially asking for anomaly detection or risk-trigger identification in model operations. We could develop and publish open benchmarks for automated risk-event detection in AI system logs, contributing to the broader scientific literature on AI safety monitoring.\n\nLet me make sure these are genuinely different:\n- Proposal 1: About MODEL ARCHITECTURE (frontier research in interpretable/traceable design)\n- Proposal 2: About DATA INFRASTRUCTURE and open science (turning compliance data into research assets)\n- Proposal 3: About EVALUATION METHODOLOGY (benchmarks for risk detection in