        "content": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"As Compliance Officer I read Article 12 as a hard operational constraint that cannot be bolted on after deployment — the automatic event-recording architecture must be designed into every high-risk system we ship, or we face enforcement action and effective exclusion from EU markets. The logging mandate means our engineering teams need to embed structured, tamper-evident capture from day one of development rather than treat it as a post-launch patch, and for Annex III 1(a) systems the prescribed minimum data fields (usage timestamps, reference databases, matched inputs, verifier identities) create specific capture obligations that may collide with current privacy-by-design assumptions and require legal review. Our institutional survival here depends on demonstrating traceability end-to-end at any point in a system's lifetime, so this is a prerequisite for continued operation, not optional overhead. I would frame the response as a targeted infrastructure and process investment rather than a strategic pivot: we continue building what we have been building, but every component now carries an audit trail by construction.\",\n\n  \"stance\": 45,\n\n  \"proposals\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Embedded Traceability Logging Layer\",\n      \"what\": \"A modular logging middleware that automatically captures Article 12(2) event categories and Annex III 1(a) minimum fields into a tamper-evident store, sitting between our inference pipeline and external interfaces so every high-risk system inherits compliance-grade recording without bespoke engineering.\",\n      \"how\": \"The platform team ships it as an internal component with Python and C++ SDKs, configures retention per system lifetime, and gates CI/CD so no build deploys without a passing logging contract test.\",\n      \"why\": \"One shared investment covers the record-keeping obligation across all products, cutting per-project compliance cost and removing the risk that one n