ent action under Article 99.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Living Technical Documentation Lifecycle System\",\n      \"what\": \"A versioned documentation repository that tracks every algorithmic revision, training-data change, and validation update across the full operational lifetime of each deployed high-risk system, fulfilling Recital 71's up-to-date requirement.\",\n      \"how\": \"We would assign a dedicated compliance engineer per product line to maintain structured technical files linked to source-repository commit hashes, and schedule quarterly gap-audits against the current regulatory checklist to flag staleness before it becomes non-conformity.\",\n      \"why\": \"Notified bodies and market-surveillance authorities will demand current documentation on inspection; gaps here are the fastest route to a finding that undermines client trust and market reputation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Post-Market Monitoring Pilot on Biometric Products\",\n      \"what\": \"A live operational monitoring programme for our Annex III(1)(a) biometric identification systems, continuously ingesting Article 12 production logs to detect performance drift, adverse-event clusters, or conditions approaching a substantial-modification threshold.\",\n      \"how\": \"We would stand up a small analytics team that consumes real-time log streams, defines quantitative trigger thresholds for risk indicators in the monitoring plan, and feeds findings into our internal incident-response workflow with documented escalation paths to senior management.\",\n      \"why\": \"Demonstrating active post-market surveillance positions the lab as a responsible market actor in regulator and client negotiations, directly safeguarding institutional survival through sustained reputation.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
