ignificant but not revolutionary. It requires changes to how systems are built (embedding logging), updating technical documentation practices, and possibly adjusting development timelines. But it's not a \"major overhaul of what the lab works on\" - it's more about HOW things are built rather than WHAT is researched. I'd say around 45-60 range. Let me think... It's mandatory compliance, so ignoring it isn't an option. But the core R&D agenda (what problems we solve) doesn't change - the engineering practices and documentation requirements do. I'll go with 52 - a moderate, balanced adjustment. Actually, let me reconsider. The regulation says \"shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events\" - this is a hard technical requirement. If the lab's current systems don't have this, they need to retrofit or redesign. But it's not changing what they research. I'll say 48 - moderate adjustment focused on engineering practices and documentation rather than fundamental research direction.\n\nNow for 3 distinct proposals:\n\n1. Something about embedding logging infrastructure into the development pipeline (engineering/technical)\n2. Something about technical documentation management and lifecycle tracking (process/documentation)\n3. Something about post-market monitoring systems or compliance testing (operational/regulatory)\n\nLet me make these genuinely different:\n- Proposal 1: Technical - Build a standardized logging module/framework that gets integrated into all high-risk AI system development from day one\n- Proposal 2: Process - Implement a technical documentation lifecycle management system to keep docs current throughout the system's lifetime\n- Proposal 3: Strategic/Market - Develop a compliance-as-a-service or certification readiness program that positions the lab as a leader in compliant AI\n\nLet me draft these carefully, keeping each between 60-110 words across all four fields combined.\n\nLet me count words carefully for each proposal.\n\nProposal 