        "content": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"Article 12 is fundamentally an architecture mandate rather than a documentation checkbox: the system must technically allow automatic event recording over its full lifetime, which means our prototyping infrastructure needs structured, append-only logging baked into every build from day one. From a speed-of-prototyping standpoint, retrofitting instrumentation after a prototype reaches functional completion is expensive and error-prone; embedding it as a mandatory SDK import in our CI pipeline costs an afternoon of setup but saves weeks of rework per project. The Annex III 1(a) minimums (start/end timestamps, reference-database pointer, matched input data, verifier identity) are specific enough to encode as schema constraints and test assertions rather than leaving them to individual engineers' judgment. My core technical-viability concern is the 'lifetime' qualifier: our current prototype stores lean on ephemeral in-memory state that would evaporate, so we need a durable storage layer that doesn't bloat every experimental run.\",\n  \"stance\": 60,\n  \"proposals\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Instrumented Prototyping SDK with Event Logging\",\n      \"what\": \"A reusable Python middleware layer that every prototype integrates at build time, automatically capturing timestamped events to a structured local store so engineers don't hand-roll instrumentation per experiment.\",\n      \"how\": \"We extract event patterns from Article 12(2) into a decorator-based API; the SDK writes append-only JSONL files and we gate CI on its import.\",\n      \"why\": \"Compliance becomes default rather than afterthought, protecting prototyping speed without sacrificing traceability.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Automated Log-Completeness Test Harness\",\n      \"what\": \"A CI test suite that, on every prototype build, replays a synthetic workload and verifies the emitted logs contain all fields mandated by Article 12(3) for our Anne