t we build), but it does require architectural changes to how we design and prototype systems. We need logging infrastructure baked in from the start, which affects our prototyping workflow. But our core AI research doesn't change - just how we package and instrument it. I'll go with 60.\n\nNow for 3 distinct proposals:\n\n1. Something about building a logging framework/SDK into our prototype stack - this is about infrastructure/tooling\n2. Something about designing the \"event taxonomy\" - what events to log, the schema design - this is about specification/design work\n3. Something about testing/validation of the logging system itself during prototyping - making sure logs are complete and correct as part of our QA cycle\n\nLet me make these genuinely different:\n- Proposal 1: Build a reusable logging instrumentation layer (SDK/middleware) into our prototyping framework - technical infrastructure\n- Proposal 2: Develop an automated compliance test harness that validates log completeness against Article 12 requirements during each prototype iteration - testing/QA angle\n- Proposal 3: Create a lightweight \"log replay\" tool that lets us simulate lifetime operation and verify traceability before deployment - operational/verification angle\n\nLet me check word counts for each proposal (title + what + how + why combined must be 60-110 words).\n\nProposal 1:\nTitle: \"Instrumented Prototyping SDK with Built-in Event Logging\" \nWhat: A reusable Python middleware layer that every prototype integrates at build time, automatically capturing timestamped events (session start/end, model version, input data hashes, decision outputs) to a structured local store. It abstracts the logging so prototyping engineers don't hand-roll instrumentation for each experiment.\nHow: We extract common event patterns from Article 12(2) and Annex III 1(a) into a small decorator-based API; the SDK writes append-only JSONL files with schema validation, and we wire it into our existing CI pipeline