uation or substantial modification retrospectively as Article 12(2)(a) demands.\nHow: We build it as a read-only query layer over the SDK's JSONL store, with a simple CLI that lets engineers run \"what happened between timestamps X and Y\" and flag gaps where traceability would break down.\nWhy: It gives us concrete evidence of technical viability for our lifetime-logging claim before regulators ask, rather than asserting it on paper.\n\nCount: Title (7) + What (~45) + How (~38) + Why (~22) = ~112. Slightly over. Let me trim.\n\nTitle: \"Lifetime Log Replay and Traceability Simulator\" \nWhat: A read-only tool that ingests accumulated prototype logs and reconstructs event timelines, letting us verify we could identify a risk situation or substantial modification retrospectively per Article 12(2)(a).\nHow: We build it as a CLI query layer over the SDK's JSONL store; engineers run timestamp-range queries and the tool flags gaps where traceability would break.\nWhy: It produces concrete evidence of lifetime-logging viability before regulators ask, not just on paper.\n\nCount: Title (7) + What (~35) + How (~28) + Why (~19) = ~89. Good.\n\nLet me recount more carefully:\n\nProposal 1:\nTitle: \"Instrumented Prototyping SDK with Event Logging\" - 7 words\nWhat: \"A reusable Python middleware layer that every prototype integrates at build time, automatically capturing timestamped events to a structured local store so engineers don't hand-roll instrumentation per experiment.\" - let me count: A(1) reusable(2) Python(3) middleware(4) layer(5) that(6) every(7) prototype(8) integrates(9) at(10) build(11) time(12), automatically(13) capturing(14) timestamped(15) events(16) to(17) a(18) structured(19) local(20) store(21) so(22) engineers(23) don't(24) hand-roll(25) instrumentation(26) per(27) experiment(28). = 28 words\nHow: \"We extract event patterns from Article 12(2) into a decorator-based API; the SDK writes append-only JSONL files and we gate CI on its import.\" - We(1) ex