        "content": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"This regulation is fundamentally an engineering-infrastructure requirement rather than a research-direction mandate, so the lab's response should centre on how we architect and deploy systems, not on what questions we investigate. My immediate concern is resource allocation: automatic logging with the specificity Article 12 demands—timestamps, input-data references, verification-personnel IDs—requires dedicated engineering capacity that currently does not exist in our project plans, yet building bespoke solutions per team would waste the very headcount and infrastructure budget I am trying to protect. The pragmatic path is to treat compliant event-recording as a shared platform capability, reusing the monitoring dashboards, log-storage nodes, and CI pipelines we already operate, so that compliance overhead is amortised across all concurrent high-risk deployments rather than duplicated in each one. This lets us preserve the scientific ambition of our R&D agenda while absorbing the regulatory cost into existing infrastructure at marginal expense.\",\n  \"stance\": 38,\n  \"proposals\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Shared Logging Middleware for High-Risk AI Deployments\",\n      \"what\": \"A common event-recording layer that plugs into every high-risk system we build, capturing usage timestamps, input-data references, and verification-personnel identifiers in one standardised schema.\",\n      \"how\": \"One senior engineer plus two juniors build it as an internal library over six months, reusing our existing monitoring dashboards and log-storage nodes rather than procuring new tooling.\",\n      \"why\": \"It amortises the compliance cost across all concurrent projects, preventing each team from reinventing the same stack and protecting our constrained headcount budget.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Embedding Compliance Gates into the R&D Project Cycle\",\n      \"what\": \"A mandatory mid-development review where each