rom the start for new projects.\n\nStance: I'd say around 35-40. It's less than the Compliance Officer's 45 because my concern is that this is an infrastructure cost, not an agenda change. The research questions stay the same, but the budget allocation shifts and existing infrastructure needs modification. Actually, let me reconsider - as Lab Director concerned with resource efficiency, I might see this slightly differently. The logging requirements don't change WHAT we study, but they do change how much funding goes to compliance vs. new discovery. And for existing infrastructure, retrofitting is costly. I'll go with 38 - it's a meaningful budget reallocation and infrastructure adjustment, but not an agenda overhaul.\n\nNow for 3 distinct proposals:\n\n1. A \"logging-first\" design pattern / template that gets embedded into all new project proposals - this addresses future projects at intake\n2. An audit/retrofit program for existing prototypes to assess which can be patched vs. which need redesign - addresses current pipeline\n3. A shared logging infrastructure service (common middleware layer) that multiple projects can use, amortizing the compliance cost across the portfolio - addresses resource efficiency and reuse\n\nLet me make sure these are genuinely different:\n- #1 is about process/design at intake (forward-looking governance)\n- #2 is about assessing and fixing what we already have (backward-looking triage)\n- #3 is about building a shared technical asset that reduces per-project compliance cost (infrastructure investment for efficiency)\n\nLet me write these out with the constraints:\n- title: max 10 words\n- what: 1-2 sentences\n- how: 1-2 sentences\n- why: 1 sentence\n- Each proposal: 60-110 words total across all four fields\n- Plain prose, no bullet lists inside fields\n\nLet me draft and count words carefully.\n\nProposal 1:\ntitle: \"Logging-First Gate for New Project Intake\" (7 words) ✓\nwhat: Every new R&D project proposal must include a loggin