        "content": "{\n\"reasoning\": \"As Compliance Officer, I read Article 12 not as a paperwork exercise but as an architectural mandate: our high-risk systems must be engineered so that they automatically and continuously log relevant events over their entire operational lifetime, and the regulation ties those logs simultaneously to risk identification (para 2(a)), post-market monitoring (para 2(b)), and operational oversight (para 2(c)). The lab must treat this as a design constraint from inception rather than a retrofit, because after deployment we cannot easily bolt on event capture without re-validating the model. For any system falling under Annex III point 1(a), the granular minimums in paragraph 3—start/end timestamps, reference database identifiers, matched input data, verifier identities—will reshape our data-pipeline schemas and access-control models specifically. Failure here is not a minor infraction; it severs the traceability chain regulators rely on to verify compliance, exposing the institution to enforcement action and severe reputational damage in the market. Our response must therefore be immediate, cross-departmental, and embedded in both engineering practice and governance structure.\",\n\"stance\": 52,\n\"proposals\": [\n{\n\"title\": \"Mandatory Event-Logging Layer for All High-Risk Pipelines\",\n\"what\": \"A standardized, embeddable logging middleware that captures system events automatically across every high-risk AI model we develop, satisfying Article 12(1) and the traceability purposes in paragraph 2.\",\n\"how\": \"The platform-engineering team will build a shared library with configurable event schemas covering risk-flag triggers, post-market monitoring signals, and operational state changes; integration becomes a mandatory item at sprint planning for every new project.\",\n\"why\": \"This single architectural decision closes the largest compliance gap in one stroke and prevents each product team from reinventing incompatible logg