a Longitudinal Behaviour Dataset\",\n\"what\": \"An internal analytics project that treats the mandatory lifetime event logs as a first-class scientific resource for studying model drift, performance degradation, and edge-case emergence across high-risk deployments.\",\n\"how\": \"We aggregate anonymised log streams from every Annex III 1(a) system in our portfolio into a versioned research corpus, then apply change-point detection and anomaly-taxonomy methods to characterise how behaviour evolves post-deployment.\",\n\"why\": \"This converts a regulatory cost centre into a unique longitudinal dataset that strengthens our publications on AI reliability and broadens the lab's external scientific impact.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Publishing Methodology for Loggable-by-Design Model Development\",\n\"what\": \"A methodological research line that formalises how to co-design model architecture and observability so that logging requirements do not degrade accuracy or constrain the hypothesis space during experimentation.\",\n\"how\": \"We run paired ablation studies—identical training regimes with and without log-driven architectural constraints—across three representative high-risk use cases, quantifying any performance delta and documenting design patterns that neutralise it.\",\n\"why\": \"This contributes a transferable methodological framework to the broader AI research community, positioning our lab as defining best practice at the compliance-science interface rather than merely complying.\"\n}\n]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this from my role as a Researcher at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act, with domain of concern: the knowledge frontier, intellectual merit of research, and broader scientific impact.\n\nMy focus is different from the Compliance Officer (who focuses on regulatory mechanics) and the Lab Director (who focuses on budget/resources). I care about:\n- The knowledge frontier: Does Article 12 constrain or redirect what we can explore scientifically?\n- Intellectual merit: Does logging-first design degrade the quality of our research?\n- Broader scientific impact: How does mandatory logging affect reproducibility, transparency, and the cumulative nature of science?\n\nLet me think about how a researcher would view this:\n\nThe key insight for my role: Article 12's logging requirements actually have a double-edged sword effect on research. On one hand, they mandate that we record events throughout the system lifetime - which could constrain exploratory, iterative research where you're constantly modifying architectures and don't yet have stable \"events\" to log. On the other hand, the requirement for comprehensible information about development, algorithms, data, training, testing processes (from Recital 71) actually aligns with good scientific practice - reproducibility, documentation of methods, etc.\n\nThe deeper concern: if logging is a hard design constraint at project intake, it could narrow the research space. For example, if we're exploring novel architectures where the \"events\" that matter aren't yet known, how do we design logging for events we haven't identified? The regulation says \"relevant to the intended purpose\" - but in frontier research, the intended purpose may shift as understanding develops.\n\nAlso: the four mandatory fields for Annex III 1(a) systems are specific and prescriptive. This means our research on those systems must be structured around these data capture points from day one, which constrains experimental design freedom.\n\nMy stance should reflect that this is a moderate adjustment - not no change (the logging constraint does affect how we structure experiments), but not a major overhaul either (we're still researching the same scientific questions). I'd put it around 42-50 range. The Compliance Officer said 45, Lab Director said 38. As a researcher focused on intellectual merit and frontier, I think this sits in a similar band - maybe slightly higher because the constraint on experimental design freedom (what events to log when you don't yet know what matters) is a genuine research constraint that doesn't exist in unconstrained scientific inquiry.\n\nLet me settle on 47 or 48. The logging requirement forces us to think about observability as a first-class research design parameter, which changes how we structure experiments but doesn't change what questions we ask. However, it does mean some exploratory approaches that rely on opaque iterative processes become harder to justify under the Act.\n\nNow for three distinct proposals:\n\n1. Something about designing a \"logging-agnostic\" experimental framework - a middleware layer that captures events generically so researchers can explore novel architectures without redesigning logging each time. This is about preserving research freedom within compliance.\n\n2. Something about using the mandatory logging data as a scientific resource - turning compliance logs into datasets for studying AI system behavior, drift detection, and performance characterization over time. This turns a constraint into an opportunity for broader impact.\n\n3. Something about publishing methodological papers on how to design loggable AI systems without degrading model quality or research throughput - contributing to the knowledge frontier by formalizing the intersection of regulatory compliance and experimental ML methodology.\n\nLet me make sure these are genuinely different:\n- #1 is an internal engineering/design tool (middleware)\n- #2 is a data science/analytics initiative (using logs as scientific input)\n- #3 is a publication/academic contribution (methodology paper on the intersection)\n\nThese are distinct: infrastructure, research output, and knowledge dissemination.\n\nNow let me write the response in proper format, keeping each proposal between 60-110 words across all four fields combined. Let me count carefully.\n\nLet me draft:\n\n(1) Reasoning (3-5 sentences):\nFrom a research standpoint, Article 12's logging mandate introduces a genuine constraint on experimental design freedom: we must now specify \"relevant events\" at project intake even when the scientific question is still being formulated, which can bias exploratory work toward pre-defined observability. However, Recital 71's emphasis on comprehensible documentation of algorithms, data, and validation processes aligns with the reproducibility standards our field has been converging toward anyway, so much of this is codifying good practice rather than imposing foreign constraints. The real intellectual risk is narrow: if we treat logging as a bolt-on after model architecture is frozen, we lose the ability to iterate on novel training or inference approaches that would naturally produce different event streams. My read is that the lab should absorb this as a design-time parameter—observability becomes a first-class variable in experimental planning—but our core scientific questions remain untouched.\n\nThat's 4 sentences. Good.\n\nStance: I'll go with 46. Slightly above the Compliance Officer because the constraint on exploratory design freedom is a research-specific concern they didn't emphasize, but still moderate overall.\n\nNow proposals - let me count words carefully for each (title + what + how + why combined = 60-110 words).\n\nProposal 1:\nTitle: \"Generic Event-Capture Middleware for Experimental ML Pipelines\" \nWhat: A lab-wide middleware layer that instruments any prototype to emit