ogical framework to the broader AI research community, positioning our lab as defining best practice at the compliance-science interface rather than merely complying. \nTotal: 9+26+35+27 = 97 words. Good.\n\nLet me double-check word counts more carefully for each proposal...\n\nProposal 1:\nTitle: \"Generic Event-Capture Middleware for Experimental ML Pipelines\" (8 words)\nWhat: A lab-wide middleware layer that instruments any prototype to emit structured, schema-flexible event logs without requiring researchers to redesign their architecture around Article 12's specific fields. (30 words) - let me recount: A(1) lab-wide(2) middleware(3) layer(4) that(5) instruments(6) any(7) prototype(8) to(9) emit(10) structured(11) schema-flexible(12) event(13) logs(14) without(15) requiring(16) researchers(17) to(18) redesign(19) their(20) architecture(21) around(22) Article(23) 12's(24) specific(25) fields(26). = 26 words\nHow: The team builds a lightweight SDK that wraps model inference and training loops, capturing timestamps, data provenance, and decision traces as append-only records; researchers configure which fields map to the four Annex III mandates at deployment time rather than design time. (39) - The(1) team(2) builds(3) a(4) lightweight(5) SDK(6) that(7) wraps(8) model(9) inference(10) and(11) training(12) loops(13) capturing(14) timestamps(15) data(16) provenance(17) and(18) decision(19) traces(20) as(21) append-only(22) records(23) researchers(24) configure(25) which(26) fields(27) map(28) to(29) the(30) four(31) Annex(32) III(33) mandates(34) at(35) deployment(36) time(37) rather(38) than(39) design(40) time(41). = 41 words\nWhy: It preserves experimental freedom during exploration while guaranteeing compliance-ready logs at commercialisation handoff. (14) - It(1) preserves(2) experimental(3) freedom(4) during(5) exploration(6) while(7) guaranteeing(8) compliance-ready(9) logs(10) at(11) commercialisation(12) handoff(13). = 13 words\n\nTotal: 8 + 26 + 41 + 13 = 