at\": \"A formal internal procedure that assigns documentation ownership, revision cadence, and version control to every high-risk system from initial concept through decommissioning, covering the technical-file requirements in Recital 71 and Article 12(2).\",\n      \"how\": \"We will designate a Documentation Lead per project, integrate living-document templates into our lab-management software, and schedule quarterly compliance reviews that verify all records—algorithms, training-data provenance, risk assessments—are current before any system ships to a partner.\",\n      \"why\": \"Maintaining up-to-date technical files protects the institution from enforcement action and preserves its eligibility for public-sector AI contracts in the EU.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Quarterly Article 12 Self-Audit and Regulator-Readiness Drills\",\n      \"what\": \"A recurring internal audit programme where compliance staff sample deployed high-risk systems, test whether logging captures the minimum fields in Article 12(3), and simulate a regulator's document request to expose gaps before they are exposed by others.\",\n      \"how\": \"Each quarter two cross-functional teams—one from engineering, one from legal-compliance—will run a table-top exercise: pick a live system, pull its logs for a random thirty-day window, verify start-and-end timestamps, database references, and verifier identifications against the record-keeping standard.\",\n      \"why\": \"Catching deficiencies internally before a market-surveillance authority finds them preserves our EU market reputation and avoids the reputational damage that would chill research partnerships.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
