eusable software middleware layer that captures system events, data lineage, and operational parameters automatically whenever a high-risk AI model enters our testing or deployment pipeline, ensuring Article 12(1) compliance is by-design rather than retrofitted. (35 words)\nHow: The engineering team will build a modular logging SDK in Q3 that hooks into existing experiment-tracking tools with configurable event schemas per Annex III category and automated export to an immutable audit store. \nWhy: Embedding this capability early avoids costly re-engineering of research prototypes and demonstrates regulatory seriousness to EU partners and funding bodies. \nTotal: ~82 words. Good.\n\nProposal 2 - Process/documentation focus:\nTitle: \"Lifetime Technical Documentation Lifecycle Programme\" \nWhat: A formal internal procedure that assigns documentation ownership, revision cadence, and version control to every high-risk system from initial concept through decommissioning, covering the technical file requirements in Recital 71 and Article 12(2). (33 words)\nHow: We will designate a Documentation Lead per project, integrate living-document templates into our lab-management software, and schedule quarterly compliance reviews that verify all records—algorithms, training data provenance, risk assessments—are current before any system ships to a partner. \nWhy: Maintaining up-to-date technical files protects the institution from enforcement action and preserves its eligibility for public-sector AI contracts in the EU. (25 words)\nTotal: ~89 words. Good.\n\nProposal 3 - Governance/audit focus:\nTitle: \"Quarterly Article 12 Self-Audit and Regulator-Readiness Drills\" \nWhat: A recurring internal audit programme where compliance staff sample deployed high-risk systems, test whether logging captures the minimum fields in Article 12(3), and simulate a regulator's document request to expose gaps. (30 words)\nHow: Each quarter, two cross-functional teams (one from engineering, one f