hitectural constraint rather than a documentation afterthought—our high-risk systems need logging capabilities engineered in from the first line of code, not retrofitted before a conformity assessment. For projects falling under Annex III point 1(a) (biometric identification), the minimum four data fields create a non-negotiable specification that our R&D teams must build into their pipelines now. I also note Recital 71's emphasis on keeping technical documentation and records \"up to date... throughout the lifetime,\" which means we need a governance structure for ongoing maintenance of logs, not just initial deployment capture. From a market-reputation standpoint, being an early demonstrator of compliance-ready logging architecture is actually a selling point with institutional clients who are also under AI Act obligations. The practical risk if we ignore this: systems that cannot produce compliant audit trails will simply be barred from the EU market for high-risk applications, which for our lab means losing our primary revenue base.\n\nThat's 5 sentences. Good.\n\nStance: 60/100. Moderate but firm adjustment. We're not changing what we research, but how we build is significantly affected. The logging architecture requirement touches every high-risk project's engineering workflow, and the biometric-specific requirements add a concrete new module to our toolchain. But core research questions remain unchanged.\n\nLet me finalize and format properly.\n",
