d, machine-generated logs - not manual documentation\n- For high-risk systems specifically (Annex III), the requirements are more granular\n\nLet me think about my stance number. Article 12 is about logging infrastructure. This isn't a fundamental change to what we build - it's an engineering discipline requirement. Any serious ML system already needs some form of logging/monitoring for production deployment. The Act formalizes and specifies what must be logged. For prototyping speed, the key insight is: if you design your prototyping stack with structured event capture from day one (rather than bolting it on), the incremental cost is low. But if we've been running throwaway prototypes without any logging discipline, that's a process change. I'd say this is maybe a 35-40 - moderate adjustment to engineering practices but not a fundamental shift in what we build or how long prototyping cycles take. The technical architecture shifts (add structured event bus), but the core R&D questions remain the same. Let me go with 38.\n\nNow for 3 distinct proposals:\n\n1. Something about building a standardized logging/telemetry SDK that slots into our prototyping pipeline - this is an infrastructure/engineering tooling project. The point is to make compliance a byproduct of normal dev workflow rather than a separate task.\n\n2. Something about redesigning our prototype-to-production transition checklist/process to embed Article 12 requirements as gate criteria. This is a process/workflow change, different from building the SDK. It's about when and how we validate that logging meets the spec before something moves forward.\n\n3. Something about prototyping a \"logging-as-specification\" approach where the event schema itself becomes part of the system design document - essentially making the log structure a first-class artifact in our architecture reviews. This is different from #1 (which is building the tool) and #2 (which is process). This is about changing how we architect sys