ker with a fixed JSON schema per event type that captures start/end times and input hashes via a single decorator call. \nWhy: Eliminates retrofitting logs post-prototype without adding meaningful boilerplate, preserving iteration velocity. \nTotal: 8 + 30 + 27 + 16 = 81 words. Good, within range.\n\nProposal 2:\nTitle: \"Log-compliance gate in the prototype-to-staging checklist\" (9 words) ✓\nWhat: A formal step inserted between our internal prototype sign-off and staging deployment where a reviewer verifies that event logs satisfy the Article 12(3) minimum fields for Annex III systems. \nHow: We add two checklists to our existing pull-request template—one mapping each required log field to its emission point in code, another confirming the append-only retention window matches intended system lifetime. \nWhy: Catches missing instrumentation while it is cheap to fix (one extra logger call) rather than after the model has already been promoted. \nTotal: 9 + 30 + 31 + 28 = 98 words. Good.\n\nProposal 3:\nTitle: \"Event-schema-first architecture templates for new models\" (8 words) ✓\nWhat: A change to how we write system design documents so that the event/log schema is drafted alongside the model I/O spec, making traceability a first-class architectural constraint rather than an afterthought. \nHow: Every new prototype kickoff doc gets a mandatory \"Event Log Schema\" section where engineers list which events will be emitted, at what granularity, and how they map to Article 12(2)(a), (b), and (c) before any code is written. \nWhy: Surfacing logging requirements during design avoids mid-sprint redesigns when a reviewer discovers an unloggable data path, protecting both compliance and schedule. \nTotal: 8 + 30 + 37 + 26 = 101 words. Good.\n\nNow let me write the reasoning (3-5 sentences) from my dev engineer perspective focused on technical viability and prototyping speed/quality:\n\nAs a dev engineer, my first reaction is that Article 12 is fundamentally an observabili