rce reallocation from our research pipeline into engineering infrastructure. It's not business as usual because the \"lifetime\" aspect means ongoing costs that weren't in our budget models. But it's not 80+ either because it doesn't change WHAT we research fundamentally - it changes how we build and maintain what we research. I'll go with 42.\n\nNow for 3 distinct, concrete proposals:\n\n1. Something about repurposing existing infrastructure - e.g., our existing experiment tracking/logging systems (like MLflow, Weights & Biases equivalents) could be extended to meet Article 12 requirements rather than building from scratch. This is about resource efficiency and using what we have.\n\n2. Something about a dedicated \"compliance engineering\" allocation within the R&D budget - carving out a specific percentage of engineering FTEs for logging architecture, so it doesn't erode research timelines unpredictably. This addresses resource-allocation efficiency directly.\n\n3. Something about phased integration tied to product lifecycle - rather than building full Article 12 compliance into every experimental model (which would kill scientific velocity), we embed the logging hooks at a specific milestone in our development pipeline, using the \"appropriate to intended purpose\" language to calibrate depth. This balances ambition with constraint.\n\nLet me make sure these are genuinely different:\n- Proposal 1: Infrastructure reuse (technical/architectural) - use existing experiment tracking\n- Proposal 2: Budget/staffing allocation (organizational/financial) - dedicated FTE carve-out\n- Proposal 3: Process/pipeline design (methodological) - phased integration at lifecycle milestones\n\nGood, these are distinct.\n\nNow let me write the response in my Lab Director voice, keeping focus on resource allocation, funding constraints, and infrastructure use.\n\nLet me count words carefully for each proposal (60-110 words total across four fields).\n\nProposal 1: \"Repurpose Experime