um(69) fields(70) mandated(71) by(72) paragraph(73) 3(74). = 40 words\n\nwhy: One(75) shared(76) backbone(77) avoids(78) each(79) project(80) reinventing(81) its(82) own(83) logging(84), which(85) is(86) how(87) we(88) stretch(89) limited(90) engineering(91) budget(92) across(93) parallel(94) grants(95) without(96) duplicating(97) cost(98). = 24 words\n\nTotal: ~98 words. That's within 60-110. Good.\n\nWait, let me be more careful with hyphenated words. \"high-risk\" - is that one word or two? Typically in word counts, hyphenated words count as one. Let me recount more carefully but I'll aim for the middle of the range.\n\n**Proposal 2: Compliance-First Sprint Planning Protocol**\n- title: \"Compliance-First Sprint Planning Protocol\" (5 words) - wait, let me make it more specific: \"Baking Article Twelve into Every Research Sprint\" (8 words) ✓\n- what: A revised project-planning protocol where each research sprint includes an explicit logging-design checkpoint before any model training or deployment work begins, ensuring traceability requirements are scoped in the first week rather than retrofitted. \n- how: I will mandate a half-day \"traceability design\" workshop at the start of every new high-risk project, staffed by one rotating compliance engineer from our shared pool, whose output becomes a binding section of the sprint's technical specification document.\n- why: Catching logging gaps before code is written costs a fraction of retrofitting them after a system enters operation, protecting our already-tight delivery timelines under fixed grant periods.\n\nLet me count:\ntitle: Baking(1) Article(2) Twelve(3) into(4) Every(5) Research(6) Sprint(7) = 7 words\nwhat: A(8) revised(9) project-planning(10) protocol(11) where(12) each(13) research(14) sprint(15) includes(16) an(17) explicit(18) logging-design(19) checkpoint(20) before(21) any(22) model(23) training(24) or(25) deployment(26) work(27) begins(28), ensuring(29) traceability(30) requirements(31) are(32) scoped(33) in(34) the(35) first(36) week(37) rather(38) than(39) retrofitted(40). = 33 words\nhow: I(41) will(42) mandate(43) a(44) half-day(45) traceability(46) design(47) workshop(48) at(49) the(50) start(51) of(52) every(53) new(54) high-risk(55) project(56), staffed(57) by(58) one(59) rotating(60) compliance(61) engineer(62) from(63) our(64) shared(65) pool(66), whose(67) output(68) becomes(69) a(70) binding(71) section(72) of(73) the(74) sprint's(75) technical(76) specification(77) document(78). = 38 words\nwhy: Catching(79) logging(80) gaps(81) before(82) code(83) is(84) written(85) costs(86) a(87) fraction(88) of(89) retrofitting(90) them(91) after(92) a(93) system(94) enters(95) operation(96), protecting(97) our(98) already-tight(99) delivery(100) timelines(101) under(102) fixed(103) grant(104) periods(105). = 27 words\n\nTotal: ~105 words. Within range. Good.\n\n**Proposal 3: Open-Source Traceability Toolkit as a Research Output**\n- title: \"Publishing an Open Traceability Toolkit for AI Labs\" (9 words) ✓\n- what: We develop and open-source a lightweight Python library that wraps any model-inference pipeline with automatic event logging, database-reference tracking, and verification-action recording aligned to Article 12 paragraph 3 minimums. \n- how: A three-person sub-team within our existing NLP group spends one quarter building the library on top of our current experiment-tracking tooling, then releases it under an MIT licence alongside a technical report we can submit for credit toward our next funding application.\n- why: The toolkit doubles as both our internal compliance layer and a citable research contribution, so the same engineering effort serves regulatory obligation, lab operations, and grant-renewal narrative simultaneously.\n\nLet me count:\ntitle: Publishing(1) an(2) Open(3) Traceability(4) Toolkit(5) for(6) AI(7) Labs(8) = 8 words\nwhat: We(9) develop(10) and(11) open-source(12) a(13) lightweight(14) Python(15) library(16) that(17) wraps(18) any(19) model-inference(20) pipeline(21) with(22) automatic(23) event(24) logging(25), database-reference(26) tracking(27), and(28) verification-action(29) recording(30) aligned(31) to(32) Article(33) 12(34) paragraph(35) 3(36) minimums(37). = 29 words\nhow: A(38) three-person(39) sub-team(40) within(41) our(42) existing(43) NLP(44) group(45) spends(46) one(47) quarter(48) building(49) the(50) library(51) on(52) top(53) of(54) our(55) current(56) experiment-tracking(57) tooling(58), then(59) releases(60) it(61) under(62) an(63) MIT(64) licence(65) alongside(66) a(67) technical(68) report(69) we(70) can(71) submit(72) for(73) credit(74) toward(75) our(76) next(77) funding(78) application(79). = 42 words\nwhy: The(80) toolkit(81) doubles(82) as(83) both(84) our(85) internal(86) compliance(87) layer(88) and(89) a(90) citable(91) research(92) contribution(93), so(94) the(95) same(96) engineering(97) effort(98) serves(99) regulatory(100) obligation(101), lab(102) operations(103), and(104) grant-renewal(105) narrative(106) simultaneously(107). = 28 words\n\nTotal: ~107 words. Within range. Good.\n\nNow let me write the 3-5 sentence reasoning paragraph from my Lab Director perspective focused on resource-allocation efficiency, balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nKey points to hit:\n- This is a compliance requirement, not a research direction change\n- But it does require engineering investment (logging infrastructure)\n- The smart move is to build shared infrastructure rather than per-project solutions (resource efficiency)\n- It's an opportunity to turn compliance into reusable tooling\n- We should integrate it into existing workflows rather than creating parallel processes\n- Our funding is constrained, so we need to make this cost-effective\n\nLet me draft:\n\n\"As Lab Director, my first read of Article 12 is that it does not redirect our scientific agenda but it does impose a non-negotiabl