s:\n\n1. **Logging-as-a-Platform service**: Build a shared, reusable logging middleware that all high-risk AI systems integrate via a standard SDK/interceptor layer. This addresses the \"technical viability\" and \"speed of prototyping\" concern directly - instead of each prototype building its own logging from scratch (slow), we have a plug-in component. The biometric-specific fields become configuration templates within this service.\n\n2. **Prototype-to-Production parity gate**: Create an engineering checklist/gate in our prototyping workflow that verifies logging capabilities are present and functional before a prototype can be marked \"technically viable\" for handoff to production. This ensures we don't discover at deployment time that the architecture doesn't support lifetime logging (which would require re-architecture - very expensive).\n\n3. **Automated technical documentation pipeline**: Build CI/CD-integrated tooling that auto-generates and updates the Recital 71-required technical documentation (algorithms, training data provenance, testing/validation records) as living documents tied to git commits, eliminating the manual batch-update workflow. This directly serves speed of prototyping because engineers don't lose time writing docs at release.\n\nLet me make sure these are genuinely different:\n- Proposal 1 is about building a shared infrastructure component (the logging layer itself)\n- Proposal 2 is about process/workflow change (a quality gate in the dev pipeline)\n- Proposal 3 is about tooling for documentation generation (different artifact, different concern)\n\nYes, these are distinct. Let me write them up with the required fields and word counts (60-110 words total across four fields per proposal).\n\nLet me draft:\n\n**Response paragraph (3-5 sentences):**\nAs a Dev Engineer, Article 12 hits my core concern directly: if logging isn't architected in from the first prototype iteration, we're looking at costly re-architecture cycles that destroy 