yer\n- Title: \"Post-Market Log Analytics Monitoring Program\" (6 words)\n- What: Establish a standing compliance function that continuously analyzes logged events against Art 12(2)(a)-(c) thresholds to detect risk situations, substantial modifications, and operational anomalies.\n- How: Assign a dedicated data analyst team to build dashboards and alerting rules on the logging infrastructure; quarterly reports feed into our Article 72 post-market monitoring file for regulators.\n- Why: Transforms passive logs into active compliance evidence, demonstrating ongoing vigilance that protects market reputation and avoids enforcement action.\n\nProposal 3: Research/development focus - studying the boundary of \"substantial modification\" through logging\n- This is about an R&D research direction shift\n- Title: \"Substantial Modification Detection via Log Drift Analysis\" (8 words)\n- What: A focused research track investigating how logged performance drift, data distribution shifts, and input pattern changes can algorithmically flag when a high-risk system has undergone a substantial modification under Article 79(1).\n- How: The ML theory group builds statistical process control models trained on historical deployment logs; outputs feed into the compliance dashboard as early-warning signals before a formal re-assessment is triggered.\n- Why: Turns a regulatory obligation into a competitive research capability that we can publish and offer to enterprise clients, strengthening both reputation and revenue.\n\nLet me check word counts for each proposal (all four fields combined must be 60-110 words):\n\nProposal 1:\nTitle: \"Standardized Event Logging Middleware for High-Risk Systems\" - let me recount: Standardized(1) Event(2) Logging(3) Middleware(4) for(5) High-Risk(6) Systems(7) = 7 words. Good, under 10.\n\nWhat: Develop an internal software framework that implements Article 12 logging requirements as a reusable component across all high-risk system projects in our pipel