ecific data fields that must be captured. I'll go with 45 — a moderate adjustment to how we engineer and document systems, but not a change in what we develop.\n\n**Three distinct proposals:**\n\nLet me make sure they're genuinely different:\n- One should be about engineering/architecture (building logging into the system design)\n- One should be about process/governance (documentation lifecycle management)\n- One should be about market/strategic positioning or a specific technical capability\n\nProposal 1: Engineering — Build an automated logging middleware layer into all high-risk AI system prototypes. This is a technical architecture change.\n\nProposal 2: Process/Governance — Create a \"Technical Documentation Lifecycle\" program that ensures documentation stays current throughout the system's lifetime (per Recital 71's \"kept up to date\" requirement). This is an organizational process change.\n\nProposal 3: Strategic/Market — Develop a \"Compliance-as-a-Feature\" certification or audit module that we can offer to downstream deployers, turning compliance into a market differentiator. Or perhaps better: establish a dedicated post-market monitoring data pipeline that leverages the mandatory logs to generate operational insights, serving both Article 72 and Article 26(5) obligations while creating value for customers.\n\nLet me refine these to be genuinely distinct:\n1. Technical architecture (build logging into systems)\n2. Organizational process (documentation lifecycle governance)\n3. Strategic/market (turn compliance data into a product feature or audit capability)\n\nNow let me write each with title, what, how, why — keeping total between 60-110 words per proposal.\n\nLet me count words carefully for each.\n\n**Proposal 1:**\nTitle: \"Embed Lifecycle Logging Middleware in All High-Risk Prototypes\" \nWhat: A shared software layer that automatically captures event timestamps, input data references, and verification-personnel identifiers into tamper-evident log