e). I'd say moderate - maybe 40-55. It's not a major overhaul of what we study, but it does shift how we conduct research and adds a layer of methodological rigor that has some intellectual value. Let me settle on around 48-52.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The researcher's concern is the knowledge frontier and broader scientific impact. Article 12:\n- Doesn't change what questions are interesting scientifically\n- Does add constraints (every experiment needs logging infrastructure)\n- Creates a new data resource (longitudinal logs) that could be mined for research insights\n- The \"technically allow\" language means we can't run unlogged prototypes easily, which slows iterative research\n- But the requirement for documenting training/testing/validation processes aligns with good scientific practice anyway\n\nI'll go with 50 - a moderate, balanced adjustment. It's not revolutionary for research direction, but it does require methodological changes and creates new analytical possibilities.\n\n(2) Three distinct proposals:\n\nProposal 1: Something about leveraging the logging infrastructure as a research tool\n- Title: \"Log-Based Drift Analysis Research Program\"\n- What: Use the mandatory lifetime logs to build a research program analyzing how high-risk AI systems degrade or shift over time\n- How: Establish standardized log schemas across all projects, then apply statistical and ML methods to detect performance drift, bias emergence, and robustness decay\n- Why: Turns a compliance obligation into a genuine knowledge contribution about AI system reliability\n\nProposal 2: Something about open methodology / reproducibility standards\n- Title: \"Open Logging Schema for Reproducible AI Research\"\n- What: Publish an open, versioned logging schema that captures the minimum Article 12 data plus additional scientifically relevant fields\n- How: Design the schema in collaboration with the compliance and engineering teams, validate it against actual system deployments, publish as a community standard\n- Why: Elevates our lab's intellectual standing by setting methodological norms for the field\n\nProposal 3: Something about the boundary between exploratory research and regulated deployment\n- Title: \"Sandbox Protocol for Unlogged Exploratory Research\"\n- What: Design an internal governance protocol that clearly delineates when a system is in \"research mode\" (pre-intended-purpose) versus \"deployment mode\" (high-risk), so exploratory work isn't strangled by logging requirements\n- How: Draft a classification framework with the compliance officer, define thresholds at which a prototype crosses into high-risk territory, and build lightweight logging that scales up rather than full infrastructure from day one\n- Why: Protects the lab's ability to pursue novel, uncertain research directions without premature compliance overhead\n\nLet me now write these out properly, keeping each proposal between 60-110 words across all four fields. Let me count carefully.\n\nLet me draft and count:\n\nProposal 1:\n- title: \"Log-Based Drift Analysis Research Program\" \n- what: We repurpose the mandatory lifetime logs as a longitudinal research corpus to study how high-risk AI systems drift, degrade, or shift in bias over their operational lifespan. This turns an engineering obligation into a genuine contribution to the science of model reliability. (43 words)\n- how: Establish a unified log schema across all eight projects in coordination with the lab director's shared-capability initiative, then apply time-series anomaly detection and statistical process control methods to the accumulated event streams. (33 words)\n- why: It generates publishable knowledge about AI system longevity that no single company would voluntarily produce, positioning us at the frontier of trustworthy-AI science. (24 words)\n\nTotal: 6 + 43 + 33 + 24 = 106 words. Good, within range.\n\nProposal 2:\n- title: \"Open Logging Schema for Reproducible AI Research\" \n- what: We