use we're not changing what science we do, just how we engineer our systems.\n\nI'll score around 55-60/100. The lab needs meaningful adjustment but not a fundamental pivot in research direction. It's about embedding compliance infrastructure into the R&D pipeline efficiently.\n\nNow for 3 distinct proposals:\n\n1. Something about refactoring existing system architectures to embed logging (infrastructure/technical)\n2. Something about resource allocation - creating a dedicated \"compliance engineering\" track or budget line that doesn't cannibalize research grants (organizational/funding)\n3. Something about leveraging existing infrastructure - perhaps building a shared logging framework/middleware across all high-risk systems to avoid redundant development (efficiency/infrastructure reuse)\n\nLet me make these genuinely different:\n- Proposal 1: Technical - retrofit/build logging middleware into current high-risk system pipeline\n- Proposal 2: Organizational/funding - restructure R&D project budgeting to include a fixed \"compliance engineering\" allocation so it doesn't compete with science\n- Proposal 3: Infrastructure reuse - create an internal shared logging service/SDK that all teams use, reducing per-project overhead\n\nLet me draft these carefully, keeping each between 60-110 words across the four fields.\n\nLet me count words carefully for each proposal.\n\n**Reasoning (3-5 sentences):**\n\nFrom my vantage point as Lab Director concerned with resource allocation and infrastructure efficiency: Article 12's automatic logging mandate is not merely a compliance checkbox but an architectural commitment that will consume engineering capacity across every high-risk system we build, diverting effort from novel research. The \"lifetime\" qualifier means this isn't a one-time integration sprint—it imposes ongoing maintenance obligations on our infrastructure budget for years after deployment. For Annex III 1(a) systems, the prescriptive data fields (timestamps, refer