at\": \"A recurring internal audit where my team simulates a regulator's technical inspection of our logging outputs against Article 12(2) and (3) for each deployed high-risk system.\",\n\"how\": \"We script scenario-based tests that trigger edge cases, substantial modifications, and post-market monitoring queries, then verify the generated logs actually contain start/end timestamps, reference-database identifiers, matched input data, and verifier identities as paragraph 3 demands.\",\n\"why\": \"Catching gaps before a notified body or market-surveillance authority does protects our market reputation and avoids enforcement actions that could halt product lines mid-launch.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Lifetime Log Retention and Retrieval Infrastructure\",\n\"what\": \"A dedicated data-governance project establishing how operational logs are stored, indexed, and made retrievable for the full operational lifetime of each high-risk system, including after decommissioning.\",\n\"how\": \"We build a tiered storage architecture with encrypted cold archives, version-controlled metadata linking every log to its specific system release, and a regulator-facing retrieval interface that produces complete event histories on demand within statutory deadlines.\",\n\"why\": \"Recital 71 explicitly ties record-keeping to post-market monitoring across the entire lifetime, so inadequate retention would leave us unable to demonstrate ongoing compliance or respond to incident investigations.\"\n}\n]\n}",
