as alongside the core algorithm, and integrate automated log-generation tests into every CI/CD pipeline before code reaches staging. (31 words)\nWhy: Without native logging capability we cannot demonstrate Article 12 compliance at audit time, and retrofitting it into mature systems is far more costly and reputationally damaging than building it in from the start. (33 words)\n\nTotal: 7+20+31+33 = 91 words. Good, within 60-110.\n\nProposal 2:\nTitle: \"Quarterly Logging Compliance Stress-Test Program\" \nWhat: A recurring internal audit where the compliance team simulates a regulator's technical inspection of our logging outputs against Article 12(2) and (3) requirements for each deployed high-risk system. (29 words)\nHow: We will script scenario-based tests—triggering edge cases, substantial modifications, and post-market monitoring queries—and verify that the generated logs actually contain start/end timestamps, reference database identifiers, matched input data, and verifier identities as Article 12(3) demands. (37 words)\nWhy: Catches gaps before a notified body or market-surveillance authority does, protecting our market reputation and avoiding enforcement actions that could halt product lines. (24 words)\n\nTotal: 6+29+37+24 = 96 words. Good.\n\nProposal 3:\nTitle: \"Lifetime Log Retention and Retrieval Infrastructure\" \nWhat: A dedicated data-governance project establishing how operational logs are stored, indexed, and made retrievable for the full operational lifetime of each high-risk system, including after decommissioning. (28 words)\nHow: We will build a tiered storage architecture with encrypted cold archives, version-controlled metadata linking logs to specific system releases, and a regulator-facing retrieval interface that can produce complete event histories on demand within statutory deadlines. (35 words)\nWhy: Recital 71 explicitly ties record-keeping to post-market monitoring across the entire lifetime, so inadequate retention would leave us unab