e establish a standing governance function that maintains version-controlled technical documentation for every high-risk system across its full operational life, keeping records of algorithms, training data lineage, validation results, and risk-management updates current as Recital 71 demands. (~38 words)\nHow: A dedicated compliance team runs quarterly documentation audits against the Article 42 technical-documentation requirements, with a central repository that logs every revision, links to corresponding code commits, and triggers alerts when records fall behind system changes. (~39 words)\nWhy: Up-to-date records are our primary evidence base if regulators request traceability verification, protecting both institutional survival and market reputation. \nTotal: 6 + 38 + 39 + 21 = 104. Good.\n\n**Proposal 3:** (Strategic/Market - turning compliance into advantage)\nTitle: \"Compliance-Ready Architecture as a Market Differentiator\" (7 words)\nWhat: We audit all existing and in-development systems against the Article 12(3) minimum logging requirements for Annex III 1(a) systems, then package our logging architecture as a verified, auditable capability that enterprise customers can cite in their own regulatory filings. (~40 words)\nHow: The compliance office partners with product marketing to produce a \"traceability certification\" brief per system, listing exactly which log fields are captured and how they map to Article 12(2)(a)-(c), turning an obligation into a sales asset. (~36 words)\nWhy: Positioning our systems as audit-ready reduces customer procurement friction in regulated sectors and differentiates us from competitors still scrambling to meet the same deadline. \nTotal: 7 + 40 + 36 + 25 = 108. Good, just under 110.\n\nLet me finalize my reasoning paragraph (3-5 sentences) and make sure it's from the Compliance Officer vantage point focused on institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nLet me draft:\n\"Article 12 is not a pap