        "role": "assistant",
        "content": "{\n\"reasoning\": \"From a knowledge-frontier and intellectual-merit standpoint, Article 12 does not alter *which* scientific questions we pursue, but it reshapes how our exploratory work is structured: every experimental iteration on a system destined for high-risk deployment becomes a permanently archived regulatory record, so the boundary between 'prototype' and 'production artefact' blurs early in a project's life. Recital 71's insistence that documentation capture algorithms, data provenance, and validation rationale in a clear, comprehensive form raises the epistemic bar on our methodological writing — we can no longer treat internal lab notes as informal, because they are the seed of a living technical file that must track every revision. The continuous 'lifetime' logging mandate also means deprecated model versions and abandoned training runs retain evidentiary value, which changes how I think about experiment hygiene and intellectual accountability across a multi-year programme. That said, the obligation is process-bound rather than substance-restricting; it governs what we record, not what we may discover, so my research agenda on frontier architectures and generalisation theory remains intact, albeit with heavier documentation discipline woven into every milestone.\",\n\"stance\": 35,\n\"proposals\": [\n{\n\"title\": \"Compliance Logs as Scientific Artefacts\",\n\"what\": \"We redesign our experiment-tracking pipeline so that the logs Article 12 mandates are structured as first-class scientific artefacts, with metadata rich enough to support independent replication and cross-lab comparison of results.\",\n\"how\": \"A small team of two researchers and one engineer builds a schema layer on top of our existing tracking infrastructure, tagging every logged event with methodological context such as hyperparameter rationale, data-split provenance, and validation-protocol version.\",\n\"why\": \"This converts a compliance burden into a publication-worthy contribution to reproducible AI research while satisfying the Article 12 recording obligation at near-zero additional marginal engineering cost.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Epistemology of Documented Learning Systems\",\n\"what\": \"A fundamental research programme asking what it means to fully document a stochastic, high-dimensional learning system — probing whether the comprehensive documentation Recital 71 envisions is epistemically achievable or necessarily lossy.\",\n\"how\": \"We frame this as a series of theoretical and empirical papers examining information-theoretic limits of model description, with case studies drawn from our own high-risk prototypes, published openly to set community standards.\",\n\"why\": \"It positions the lab at the knowledge frontier of AI interpretability while directly informing how tightly our internal documentation can and must be written.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Privacy-Preserving Logs for Biometric Verification Chains\",\n\"what\": \"A targeted methodology project developing differential-privacy and verifiable-computation techniques that let us satisfy the four minimum logging fields in Article 12(3) without storing raw biometric templates on our infrastructure.\",\n\"how\": \"We collaborate with a cryptography group to prototype audit trails where regulators can confirm match events occurred without us retaining identifiable input data, validated against Annex III point 1(a) requirements.\",\n\"why\": \"It protects research subjects' data while keeping our biometric-robustness experiments viable under the Act's strictest logging tier.\"\n}\n]\n}",
