notations are captured in a unified, queryable format suitable for statistical analysis rather than mere regulatory filing.\",\n      \"why\": \"This turns a compliance cost into a unique scientific asset no competitor possesses at our scale, directly advancing the knowledge frontier on how AI systems actually behave outside controlled laboratory conditions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Dual-Purpose Logging for Compliance and Reproducibility\",\n      \"what\": \"Redesign our internal experiment-tracking and system-monitoring pipelines so that every log entry simultaneously satisfies Article 12 traceability mandates and meets the reproducibility standards our research publications require, eliminating duplicate documentation effort.\",\n      \"how\": \"Define a single logging ontology mapping each required field—start-end timestamps, reference-database identifiers, matched input records, verifier IDs—to corresponding metadata fields in our existing experiment notebooks and version-control system, then audit quarterly against notified-body expectations.\",\n      \"why\": \"This protects intellectual merit by ensuring no scientific result we publish lacks the operational traceability to withstand peer or regulatory scrutiny, strengthening the credibility of our broader output.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Open-Source Framework for AI Observability Science\",\n      \"what\": \"Initiate a publishable research programme that formalises the scientific questions behind effective logging of high-risk AI systems—optimal event granularity, drift-detection thresholds, minimal sufficient log schemas—as a contribution to the field's methodological literature.\",\n      \"how\": \"Convene a small cross-lab working group to benchmark different logging strategies against real incident data from our deployments, then release an open-source reference implementation and associated paper in a peer-reviewed venue on trustworthy AI.\",\n      \"why\": \"Positioning our