        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to respond as a Researcher at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act, specifically focused on Article 12 (Record-keeping). My domain of concern is: the knowledge frontier, intellectual merit of research, and broader scientific impact.\n\nLet me think about how a pure researcher would view this regulation:\n\nA researcher's primary concern is advancing knowledge, publishing findings, pushing the intellectual frontier. Article 12 requires automatic logging of events over the lifetime of high-risk AI systems. From a research perspective:\n\n1. The logging requirements (especially paragraph 3 for biometric systems) create data-recording obligations that intersect with research methodology. In ML/AI research, we already track experiments, but \"automatic recording of events\" in a production system is different from experiment tracking.\n\n2. The requirement to log \"input data for which the search has led to a match\" and \"the reference database against which input data has been checked\" raises interesting questions about reproducibility and scientific rigor. Actually, this could be seen as *helping* research because it creates better documentation of what systems do.\n\n3. The \"lifetime\" requirement means logging continues even after a system is deployed - this affects how we think about the relationship between our research prototypes and their deployment lifecycle.\n\n4. From a scientific impact perspective: if logging data accumulates over time, it could create valuable datasets for understanding AI system behavior, drift, failure modes - which is itself a research opportunity.\n\n5. The risk to research: compliance overhead could divert resources away from frontier work. But the researcher's stance should be nuanced - they care about intellectual merit and scientific impact more than operational compliance per se.\n\nMy stance: As a Researcher focused on knowledge frontier and scientific