urpose: regulatory compliance AND research reproducibility/auditability of results. This changes HOW we document experiments.\n\n3. Something about publishing open research on the science of effective AI system monitoring/logging - i.e., treating the technical challenge of \"what events to log, how to structure them for maximum diagnostic value\" as itself a publishable research question that advances the field beyond what any single lab needs. This is broader scientific impact.\n\nLet me make sure these are genuinely different:\n- Proposal 1: Use compliance data FOR research (data-as-corpus)\n- Proposal 2: Redesign logging to serve both compliance AND reproducibility standards (methodological change)\n- Proposal 3: Publish open research on the science of AI system observability/monitoring (field-level contribution)\n\nYes, these are distinct. Let me write them up in the required format, keeping each between 60-110 words across all four fields combined.\n\nLet me draft and count words carefully.\n\nProposal 1:\nTitle: \"Compliance Logs as a Behavioral Research Corpus\" \nWhat: Reframe the Article 12 automatic event logs generated by our deployed high-risk systems into a longitudinal research dataset for studying model drift, failure-mode emergence, and real-world performance degradation over months and years of operation. (30 words)\n\nHow: Partner with our data-engineering team to standardize log schemas across all Annex III deployments so that timestamps, input matches, reference-database versions, and verifier annotations are captured in a unified, queryable format suitable for statistical analysis rather than mere regulatory filing. (36 words)\n\nWhy: This turns a compliance cost into a unique scientific asset no competitor possesses at our scale, directly advancing the knowledge frontier on how AI systems actually behave outside controlled lab conditions. \nTotal: 8 + 30 + 36 + 25 = 99 words. Good.\n\nProposal 2:\nTitle: \"Dual-Purpose Logging for Compliance and Reproducibility\" \nWhat: Redesign our internal experiment-tracking and system-monitoring pipelines so that every log entry simultaneously satisfies Article 12's traceability mandate and meets the reproducibility standards our research publications require, eliminating duplicate documentation work. (30 words)\n\nHow: Define a single logging ontology that maps each required field—start/end timestamps, reference-database identifiers, matched input records, verifier IDs—to corresponding metadata fields in our existing experiment notebooks and version-control system, then audit quarterly against notified-body expectations. (37 words)\n\nWhy: This protects intellectual merit by ensuring no scientific result we publish lacks the operational traceability to withstand peer or regulatory scrutiny, strengthening the credibility of our broader output. \nTotal: 8 + 30 + 37 + 26 = 101 words. Good.\n\nProposal 3:\nTitle: \"Open-Source Framework for AI System Observability Science\" \nWhat: Initiate a publishable research programme that formalizes the scientific questions behind effective logging of high-risk AI systems—optimal event granularity, drift-detection thresholds, minimal sufficient log schemas—as a contribution to the field's methodological literature. (34 words)\n\nHow: Convene a small cross-lab working group to benchmark different logging strategies against real incident data from our deployments, then release an open-source reference implementation and associated paper in a peer-reviewed venue on trustworthy AI. (34 words)\n\nWhy: Positioning our lab as the source of the field's monitoring methodology amplifies broader scientific impact far beyond our own compliance needs and establishes intellectual leadership. \nTotal: 8 + 34 + 34 + 25 = 101 words. Good.\n\nNow let me write the 3-5 sentence reasoning paragraph from the researcher's vantage point, then give the stance number, then the three proposals.\n\nLet me refine my stance: As a Researcher focused on knowledg