ery prototype template\n- What: A reusable instrumentation layer embedded in our base prototype architecture that captures structured events (timestamps, pipeline stage identifiers, input/output hashes) automatically on every run.\n- How: We extend our existing experiment-tracking framework with an event-emitter middleware that hooks into the request lifecycle and writes to a time-series store; engineers pull it via a single dependency in their project scaffold.\n- Why: This removes per-project logging design overhead and ensures no high-risk prototype ships without the Article 12(1) automatic-recording capability, protecting prototyping speed.\n\nLet me count words... \"A reusable instrumentation layer embedded in our base prototype architecture that captures structured events (timestamps, pipeline stage identifiers, input/output hashes) automatically on every run.\" - that's about 30 words for \"what\"\n\"We extend our existing experiment-tracking framework with an event-emitter middleware that hooks into the request lifecycle and writes to a time-series store; engineers pull it via a single dependency in their project scaffold.\" - about 37 words for \"how\"\n\"This removes per-project logging design overhead and ensures no high-risk prototype ships without the Article 12(1) automatic-recording capability, protecting prototyping speed.\" - about 24 words for \"why\"\n\nTotal: ~91 words. Good, within 60-110.\n\nProposal 2: \"Log-Schema Acceptance Gates in CI/CD\"\n- What: A set of automated test cases added to our CI pipeline that verify every high-risk system build emits the four Annex III biometric data fields (use timestamps, reference-database ID, matched inputs, verifier identity) before a release can proceed.\n- How: We write schema-validation checks and synthetic end-to-end tests that trigger a full verification flow against a stub database, then assert all four Article 12(3) fields appear in the log output with correct types and non-null values.\n- Why: This catches missing or malformed logging in early sprints rather than at notified-body review, preventing costly rework and keeping prototype iteration velocity intact.\n\nLet me count... What: ~40 words. How: ~35 words. Why: ~25 words. Total ~100. Good.\n\nProposal 3: \"Biometric Event-Capture as Detachable Sidecar\"\n- What: A standalone logging microservice that attaches to any biometric verification prototype via a lightweight API contract, capturing the four mandated data fields without requiring changes to the model's inference code.\n- How: We define an OpenAPI specification for the sidecar's input schema (session start/end, DB reference pointer, matched-record hash, verifier auth token), implement it in our service mesh, and let each prototype team wire their pipeline through it with a single configuration flag.\n- Why: This decouples compliance instrumentation from algorithm development so engineers can swap or upgrade models without re-validating the logging path, preserving prototyping agility across system lifetimes.\n\nCount... What: ~35 words. How: ~40 words. Why: ~28 words. Total ~103. Good.\n\nLet me verify these are genuinely different:\n1. Infrastructure/template - a reusable code scaffold in the base architecture\n2. Process/quality - test gates in CI that enforce compliance before release\n3. Architecture pattern for biometric specifically - a detachable service that handles the 4 fields without touching model code\n\nYes, these are distinct: one is about default code structure, one is about testing/verification process, one is about a specific architectural decoupling pattern for the hardest case (biometric). Good.\n\nNow let me write my reasoning in 3-5 sentences from my Dev Engineer perspective.\n",
