posal 2:\nTitle: \"Log Schema Contract as Build-Time Gate\" (7 words)\nWhat: A JSON-schema registry that every high-risk system's output pipeline must conform to at CI time, encoding the Article 12(3)(a)-(d) minimum fields as non-negotiable type constraints.\nHow: We publish the schema in our internal package registry; CI fails any pull request whose log payload doesn't validate against it, and a versioning policy handles forward-compatible migrations across retraining cycles.\nWhy: Gaps are caught at commit time rather than discovered during a regulatory audit, protecting both schedule and system integrity.\n\nCount: Title(7) + What(~28) + How(~30) + Why(~19) = ~84 words. Good.\n\nProposal 3:\nTitle: \"Production Logs as Regression Test Fixtures\" (7 words)\nWhat: A pipeline that samples anonymised operational logs from deployed high-risk systems and replays them through pre-release model versions to detect behavioural drift before shipping updates.\nHow: We extend our nightly CI with a log-replay job that ingests the last 30 days of production events, runs them against candidate builds, and flags divergence beyond tolerance thresholds.\nWhy: Turns a compliance obligation into continuous quality assurance, catching edge-case regressions that static test suites structurally cannot reach.\n\nCount: Title(7) + What(~28) + How(~29) + Why(~19) = ~83 words. Good.\n\nLet me verify the proposals are genuinely different:\n1. A developer tool (middleware library) — enables engineers to comply by default during prototyping\n2. An architecture/governance standard (schema registry + CI gate) — enforces consistency across the portfolio\n3. A QA process integration (log-replay in testing) — leverages the mandated data to improve release quality\n\nYes, these are distinct: tooling vs. standard vs. process. Good.\n\nLet me finalize and make sure my tone is that of a Dev Engineer who cares about technical viability and prototyping speed/quality.\n",
