 I'd say around 35-40. It's not a major overhaul of what we work on, but it does require meaningful adjustments to how we build and maintain systems. The logging infrastructure is an engineering add-on that changes resource allocation (storage, maintenance staff, ongoing costs). But our core research questions don't change. I'll go with 35 - it's a moderate adjustment, leaning toward the lower end because the core science doesn't change but the engineering pipeline does.\n\nNow for 3 distinct proposals:\n\n1. Something about logging infrastructure - building a unified logging framework into existing R&D pipelines. This is about effective use of existing infrastructure.\n\n2. Something about long-term data retention and lifecycle management - the \"lifetime\" aspect means we need to think about storage costs, data governance over years. This is about resource-allocation efficiency.\n\n3. Something about post-market monitoring capabilities built into our testing/evaluation framework - rather than building separate compliance systems, we integrate monitoring into our existing evaluation pipelines. This balances scientific ambition (we're already doing evaluation) with regulatory requirements.\n\nLet me make these genuinely different:\n- Proposal 1: Engineering/architecture - unified logging middleware integrated into deployment pipeline\n- Proposal 2: Financial/operational planning - lifecycle cost modeling and storage architecture for the \"over lifetime\" requirement\n- Proposal 3: Research methodology shift - embedding post-market monitoring as a research question, turning compliance into scientific output\n\nLet me write these out with the format requirements:\n- title: max 10 words\n- what: 1-2 sentences\n- how: 1-2 sentences\n- why: 1 sentence\n- Each proposal between 60 and 110 words across all four fields combined\n- Plain prose, no bullet lists inside fields\n- Stay in role\n\nLet me draft and count words carefully.\n\nProposal 1: \"Unified Logging Middleware 