re not changing WHAT we research - we're changing HOW we engineer certain systems. It's an infrastructure investment, not a pivot in scientific direction.\n\nLet me settle on 58/100. Moderate-to-significant adjustment: we need to restructure our engineering pipeline and allocate dedicated resources, but our core research programs remain intact.\n\nNow for the reasoning (3-5 sentences):\n- The automatic logging requirement is an architecture-level constraint, not a documentation task - it means engineering time must be allocated upfront\n- As Lab Director, I see this as an infrastructure investment that can be amortized across multiple projects rather than a per-project tax\n- The Annex III 1(a) specifics are manageable; the broader Article 12(2) requirements need careful scoping to avoid over-engineering\n- We should not let compliance engineering cannibalize research time, but we must build shared logging infrastructure now before it becomes a deployment bottleneck\n- Our existing data pipelines and monitoring tooling give us head start - this is more about formalizing and extending what exists\n\nThree proposals that are genuinely different:\n\n1. **Infrastructure/Engineering**: Build a shared automated logging framework as reusable middleware that plugs into all high-risk AI system deployments. This is an engineering infrastructure project.\n\n2. **Process/Governance**: Restructure the R&D project pipeline to embed compliance-by-design gates at specific milestones (architecture review, pre-deployment). This changes HOW projects flow through the lab, not what they do technically.\n\n3. **Research/Scientific**: Fund a small internal research stream on efficient logging architectures - how to make comprehensive event recording computationally lightweight so it doesn't degrade system performance or consume disproportionate resources. This is actually a scientific contribution that also serves compliance needs and could be publishable/fundable.\n\nLet me check these are genuinely different:\n1 = Build the tool (engineering asset)\n2 = Change the process pipeline (governance/workflow change)\n3 = Do research ON the problem itself (scientific investment)\n\nYes, those are distinct. Let me write them up with word counts between 60-110 words across all four fields.\n\nLet me draft and count:\n\nProposal 1: \"Shared Logging Middleware for High-Risk Deployments\"\n- title: Shared Logging Middleware for High-Risk Deployments \n- what: A reusable event-recording library integrated into our deployment pipeline that auto-captures timestamps, reference-database identifiers, input matches, and verifier identities required under Article 12(3). (26)\n- how: The systems-engineering team extends our existing CI/CD monitoring stack with a plugin-based logging adapter; each high-risk project links to it at the architecture-review gate rather than building bespoke loggers. (30)\n- why: Amortising one engineering investment across every high-risk system avoids duplicated per-project work and keeps R&D staff focused on model development rather than compliance plumbing. (25)\n\nTotal: 6+26+30+25 = 87 words. Good, within 60-110.\n\nProposal 2: \"Compliance Milestones in the Project Pipeline\"\n- title: Embed Compliance Gates in R&D Project Milestones \n- what: A restructuring of our stage-gate review process so that log-schema design, retention-policy sign-off, and technical-documentation currency checks are mandatory checkpoints before a project can advance to deployment or external partnership. (31)\n- how: The lab management board updates the existing project-review calendar; each gate gains one additional reviewer slot dedicated to record-keeping adequacy, funded from our current administrative budget rather than new headcount. (29)\n- why: This prevents late-stage compliance surprises that stall deployments and force expensive rework, protecting both timeline predictability and grant-funder confidence in our delivery capacity. (24)\n\nTotal: 6+31