stem class, then release a de-identified sample corpus to three partner universities under a data-use agreement.\",\n      \"why\": \"This extends our lab's broader scientific impact beyond our own products by creating a shared evidentiary base for the AI-reliability community, elevating our institutional voice in both research and regulatory discourse.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this from my role as a Researcher at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act, with domain of concern: the knowledge frontier, intellectual merit of research, and broader scientific impact.\n\nThe regulation (Article 12) requires:\n- Automatic event logging over system lifetime for high-risk AI systems\n- Logging capabilities for traceability appropriate to intended purpose\n- Specific minimum data fields for Annex III point 1(a) biometric systems (recording periods, reference databases, input data matches, identification of verification persons)\n\nFrom my research vantage point, I need to think about:\n- How this affects the knowledge frontier\n- Intellectual merit of research\n- Broader scientific impact\n\nLet me reason through how the lab should respond from a RESEARCH perspective:\n\n1. Article 12's logging requirements are not just compliance checkboxes - they create a rich data substrate that can itself be a source of scientific insight. The \"events\" logged over system lifetimes constitute longitudinal operational data that could inform research on AI degradation, drift, failure modes, and emergent behaviors. This is genuinely at the knowledge frontier.\n\n2. Recital 71 emphasizes \"how high-risk AI systems have been developed and how they perform throughout their lifetime\" - this lifetime performance data is exactly what's missing from current ML research. Most papers report on training and benchmark performance, not on long-term operational behavior. The logging mandate essentially creates a mandatory longitudinal study infrastructure.\n\n3. From an intellectual merit standpoint, the requirement that logs capture \"situations that may result in risk\" (Art 12(2)(a)) pushes toward developing novel detection methods for anomalous system behavior - this is a genuine research frontier in AI safety and reliability.\n\n4. The broader scientific impact: if we treat the logging infrastructure