sk system we develop.\n- How: The engineering team builds and maintains an internal logging SDK with configurable schemas covering Annex III 1(a) minimum fields, tested against the full lifetime scenarios before any system ships.\n- Why: Eliminates per-project compliance risk, reduces time-to-market for new systems, and provides a single auditable artifact for regulators.\n\nLet me count words... I need to be between 60-110 words across all four fields for each proposal. Let me draft carefully.\n\nProposal 2: Process/Documentation - Living technical documentation system\n- This is about the documentation/process side (Recital 71 emphasizes keeping docs up-to-date throughout lifetime)\n- Title: \"Living Technical Documentation Lifecycle Program\" \n- What: A structured program that treats technical documentation as a continuously maintained living artifact rather than a one-time deliverable, ensuring it tracks every algorithmic change, data update, and performance shift across the system's operational life.\n- How: Assign dedicated documentation engineers to each high-risk product line who maintain versioned records of training data provenance, validation results, and risk-management decisions, with automated triggers that flag when a code or model change requires doc updates before deployment.\n- Why: Directly satisfies Recital 71's requirement for up-to-date technical documentation and positions the lab as a transparent, auditable partner in eyes of regulators and enterprise clients.\n\nLet me count words carefully for each field and total.\n\nProposal 3: Research/Strategic - Post-market monitoring research\n- This is about the R&D direction itself - investing in post-market monitoring capabilities as a research area\n- Title: \"Post-Market Monitoring Analytics Research Line\" \n- What: A dedicated research track focused on developing methods for continuous operational monitoring and anomaly detection in deployed high-risk systems, feeding directly into Article 12(