        "role": "assistant",
        "content": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"Article 12 is fundamentally an engineering-infrastructure obligation rather than a mandate to alter our research questions, so the lab's primary response should be to retrofit automatic logging and structured event capture into the AI systems we already maintain, reusing our existing observability pipelines wherever possible. From a resource-allocation standpoint, the smartest move is to treat the logging layer as shared infrastructure: one-time build cost amortised across every high-risk deployment rather than bolting on bespoke compliance code project by project. The documentation and record-maintenance duties echoed in Recital 71 do introduce a recurring process overhead that I will absorb through protected time allocations within current headcount, not through new hiring, to keep our burn rate stable heading into the next funding cycle. What this does require of me is a modest re-scoping of Q4 engineering sprints away from two speculative features and toward hardening the telemetry stack, which I can justify to the board as protecting downstream revenue from non-compliance exposure. In sum, the lab bends its build process without bending its science.\",\n  \"stance\": 35,\n  \"proposals\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Embed unified telemetry layer in existing stack\",\n      \"what\": \"A cross-system logging module that automatically records usage periods, reference databases, matched inputs, and verifier identities into a central event store, satisfying the Article 12(3) minimums for Annex III point 1(a) systems.\",\n      \"how\": \"The platform engineering team refactors our current microservice observability pipelines to capture the four mandatory data points at inference time, reusing existing Kafka-based streaming infrastructure rather than procuring new tooling.\",\n      \"why\": \"This converts a compliance obligation into a one-time infrastructure investment that amortises across every future high-risk deploym