n open documentation framework or data standard that could serve the broader community (broader scientific impact angle)\n\nLet me make sure these are genuinely different:\n- Proposal 1: Methodological - changing how we design and run experiments to incorporate logging from day one\n- Proposal 2: New research direction - studying the science of AI system lifetime behavior, drift, degradation as a frontier question\n- Proposal 3: Infrastructure/standards - building an open-source or shared framework for compliant technical documentation that benefits the field\n\nLet me draft these and count words carefully. Each proposal must be 60-110 words total across title + what + how + why.\n\nProposal 1: \"Embedding Traceability into Experimental Design Protocols\"\nTitle: \"Traceability-by-Design in AI Experimentation\" (5 words) - good, under 10\nWhat: We restructure our experimental pipelines so that every high-risk model run automatically generates a complete event log from first training epoch through deployment, rather than treating logging as a post-hoc compliance step.\nHow: I would lead a working group to redefine our standard operating procedures for model development, baking in structured logging of data provenance, hyperparameter choices, and validation checkpoints at each stage of the pipeline.\nWhy: This preserves the intellectual integrity of our research by making every result fully reproducible and auditable while simultaneously satisfying Article 12's traceability mandate without slowing down publication timelines.\n\nLet me count words: Title (5) + What (~30) + How (~28) + Why (~27) = ~90 words. Good, within 60-110.\n\nProposal 2: \"Lifetime Drift as a Frontier Research Question\"\nTitle: \"Researching AI System Lifetime Behavioral Drift\" (6 words) - good\nWhat: We open a new research track studying how high-risk AI systems' outputs evolve and degrade over their operational lifetime, treating the mandatory continuous logging data as a rich empirical resour