u can't retrofit \"automatic recording of events over the lifetime\" easily), but it doesn't change WHAT we build, just HOW we structure and instrument our prototypes. For a prototyping-focused lab, this adds overhead to every project cycle but once the infrastructure is in place, subsequent projects benefit. I'll go with 58.\n\nNow for 3 distinct proposals:\n\n1. Something about building a logging/observability SDK or framework into the prototyping toolchain - this is an infrastructure/architecture change. Think of it as making compliance-by-design a first-class part of our dev environment.\n\n2. Something specific to the Annex III 1(a) requirements - maybe building a reference validation pipeline that automatically captures the required data points (timestamps, database references, input matches, verification personnel). This is more about a specific product feature/module.\n\n3. Something about prototyping methodology change - maybe a \"compliance-first\" prototyping sprint where the first milestone is always the event schema and logging architecture before any ML model work begins. Or perhaps building an automated log-audit tool that validates prototypes against Article 12 requirements during CI/CD.\n\nLet me make sure these are genuinely different:\n- Proposal 1: Infrastructure/toolchain (build the logging SDK into our dev platform)\n- Proposal 2: A specific technical module for biometric verification systems (the Annex III 1(a) data capture pipeline)\n- Proposal 3: Process/methodology change in how we run prototyping sprints (compliance-gated milestones in CI/CD)\n\nThese are distinct: one is infrastructure, one is a product-level feature, one is process.\n\nNow let me write them keeping each between 60-110 words across all four fields. Let me count carefully.\n\nProposal 1:\nTitle: \"Embed Structured Event Logging in the Dev SDK\" \nWhat: A shared Python/TypeScript SDK that every prototype imports from day one, providing a standard event-emitter interface with schema validation for timestamped records, system state snapshots, and input/output capture. (27 words)\nHow: The platform team builds the SDK as an internal library, defines JSON-Schema templates per risk category, and wires it into our CI pipeline so any prototype that fails to emit required events blocks on merge. (30 words)\nWhy: It converts Article 12 from a per-project afterthought into zero-cost scaffolding, protecting our prototyping speed by removing the retrofit problem entirely. \nTotal: ~92 words. Good, within range.\n\nProposal 2:\nTitle: \"Biometric Verification Data-Capture Module\" \nWhat: A standalone microservice that sits between the biometric matcher and the verification UI, automatically recording start/end timestamps of each session, the reference database version hash, matched input vectors, and the verifying officer's ID per Annex III 1(a). (35 words)\nHow: We implement it as an OpenTelemetry-compatible collector with a pluggable storage backend, prototype against synthetic biometric datasets in two sprints, then integrate into our existing verification pipeline. (27 words)\nWhy: It gives us a reusable, compliance-ready component for any Annex III 1(a) engagement without rebuilding the capture layer from scratch each time. \nTotal: ~95 words. Good.\n\nProposal 3:\nTitle: \"Compliance-Gated Prototyping Sprint Template\" \nWhat: A revised sprint cadence where milestone one is always the event-schema definition and logging-architecture spike, before any model training or feature development begins, enforced through a CI/CD gate that rejects builds missing required log endpoints. (35 words)\nHow: We update our internal agile playbook, add a \"log-contract\" artifact to every project board, and write a linter plugin for our build system that checks emitted events against the Article 12 checklist. (30 words)\nWhy: It front-loads the architectural cost so downstream sprints stay fast, keeping our speed-to-prototype advantage intact while eliminating late-stage compliance rework. \nTotal: ~99 words. Good.\n\nLet me recount more carefully for each:\n\nProposal 1:\nTitle: \"Embed Structured Event Logging in the Dev SDK\" - this is just a title, not counted? Let me re-read the instructions. \"Keep each proposal between 60 and 110 words across its four fields.\" The four fields are what, how, why... wait, that's three. Title + what + how + why = four fields. Let me count all text including title.\n\nActually, let me re-read: \"For each proposal give: title, what, how, why\" and \"Keep each proposal between 60 and 110 words across its four fields.\"\n\nSo I count words in title + what + how + why together.\n\nLet me recount Proposal 1:\nTitle (9) + What (27) + How (30) + Why (20) = ~86. Let me be more precise.\n\n\"Embed Structured Event Logging in the Dev SDK\" = 9 words\n\"A shared Python/TypeScript SDK that every prototype imports from day one, providing a standard event-emitter interface with schema validation for timestamped records, system state snapshots, and input/output capture.\" = let me count: A(1) shared(2) Python/TypeScript(3) SDK(4) that(5) every(6) prototype(7) imports(8) from(9) day(10) one(11), providing(12) a(13) standard(14) event-emitter(15) interface(16) with(17) schema(18) validation(19) for(20) timestamped(21) records(22), system(23) state(24) snapshots(25), and(26) input/output(27) capture(28). = 28 words\n\"The platform team builds the SDK as an internal library, defines JSON-Schema templates per risk category, and wires it into our CI pipeline so any prototype that fails to emit required events blocks on merge.\" = The(1) platform(2) team(3) builds(4) the(5) SDK(6) as(7) an(8) internal(9) library(10), defines(11) JSON-Schema(12) templates(13) per(14) risk(15) category(16), and(17) wires(18) it(19) into(20) our(21) CI(22) pipeline(23) so(24) any(25) prototype(26) that(27) fails(28) to(29) emit(30) required(31) events(32) blocks(33) on(34) merge(35). = 35 words\n\"It converts Article 12 fr