35) paragraph(36) three(37). = 37 words\n\n\"We implement it as an OpenTelemetry-compatible collector with a pluggable storage backend, prototype against synthetic biometric datasets in two sprints, then integrate into our existing verification pipeline.\"\nWe(1) implement(2) it(3) as(4) an(5) OpenTelemetry-compatible(6) collector(7) with(8) a(9) pluggable(10) storage(11) backend(12), prototype(13) against(14) synthetic(15) biometric(16) datasets(17) in(18) two(19) sprints(20), then(21) integrate(22) into(23) our(24) existing(25) verification(26) pipeline(27). = 27 words\n\n\"It gives us a reusable, compliance-ready component for any Annex III biometric engagement without rebuilding the capture layer from scratch each time.\"\nIt(1) gives(2) us(3) a(4) reusable(5), compliance-ready(6) component(7) for(8) any(9) Annex(10) III(11) biometric(12) engagement(13) without(14) rebuilding(15) the(16) capture(17) layer(18) from(19) scratch(20) each(21) time(22). = 22 words\n\nTotal: 5 + 37 + 27 + 22 = 91. Good.\n\nProposal 3:\n\"Compliance-Gated Prototyping Sprint Template\" = Compliance(1) Gated(2) Prototyping(3) Sprint(4) Template(5) = 5 words (or 6 if compliance-gated is two). Let's say 6.\n\n\"A revised sprint cadence where milestone one is always the event-schema definition and logging-architecture spike before any model training begins, enforced through a CI gate that rejects builds missing required log endpoints.\"\nA(1) revised(2) sprint(3) cadence(4) where(5) milestone(6) one(7) is(8) always(9) the(10) event-schema(11) definition(12) and(13) logging-architecture(14) spike(15) before(16) any(17) model(18) training(19) begins(20), enforced(21) through(22) a(23) CI(24) gate(25) that(26) rejects(27) builds(28) missing(29) required(30) log(31) endpoints(32). = 32 words\n\n\"We update the internal agile playbook, add a log-contract artifact to every project board, and write a linter plugin for our build system that checks emitted events against the Article 12 checklist.\"\nWe(1) update(2) the(3) internal(4) agile(5) playbook(6), add(7) a(8) log-contract(9) artifact(10) to(11) every(12) project(13) board(14), and(15) write(16) a(17) linter(18) plugin(19) for(20) our(21) build(22) system(23) that(24) checks(25) emitted(26) events(27) against(28) the(29) Article(30) 12(31) checklist(32). = 32 words\n\n\"It front-loads the architectural cost so downstream sprints stay fast, keeping our speed-to-prototype advantage while eliminating late-stage compliance rework.\"\nIt(1) front-loads(2) the(3) architectural(4) cost(5) so(6) downstream(7) sprints(8) stay(9) fast(10), keeping(11) our(12) speed-to-prototype(13) advantage(14) while(15) eliminating(16) late-stage(17) compliance(18) rework(19). = 19 words\n\nTotal: 6 + 32 + 32 + 19 = 89. Good, within range.\n\nNow let me write the reasoning section (3-5 sentences):\n\nFrom a prototyping and technical viability standpoint, Article 12 is primarily an architectural constraint rather than a new research problem—it mandates that our systems be instrumented for automatic event logging from inception, which means we cannot defer observability to a post-mvp phase. The practical risk to the lab is speed: if every prototype must retroactively add structured logging, iteration cycles bloat and we lose our competitive edge in rapid prototyping. However, treated as a one-time infrastructure investment (a shared logging SDK plus CI gates), the per-project overhead becomes negligible after the initial build-out. For Annex III 1(a) biometric work specifically, the mandatory data-capture fields are concrete enough to spec out immediately and fold into existing pipeline components. My recommendation is to absorb this as a platform-layer change rather than a project-by-project compliance burden.\n\nThat's 5 sentences. Good.\n\nStance number: I'll go with 58. It's more than moderate because it changes the architecture of every system we build, but less than a major overhaul because it doesn't change what we researc