tions of compliant-by-construction design, strengthening both regulatory standing and intellectual credibility in system-level AI research.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Log-Derived Risk Signals for Emergent Failure Modes\",\n\"what\": \"Launch a research programme that treats the mandatory event streams as a new scientific data source, developing statistical and machine-learning methods to detect subtle drift, distribution shift, or compounding error patterns in long-running high-risk systems before they manifest as failures.\",\n\"how\": \"Partner with our post-market monitoring team to access anonymised operational logs from deployed systems, then build supervised and self-supervised anomaly-detection baselines that researchers can benchmark against, publishing methods as open-access papers.\",\n\"why\": \"This opens a genuine frontier in longitudinal AI reliability science while producing practical tools that satisfy Article 12's traceability intent beyond mere compliance checkboxes.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Open Reproducibility Schema for AI System Documentation\",\n\"what\": \"Develop and release an open, machine-readable documentation schema that standardises how we record algorithm choices, data lineage, training hyperparameters, validation protocols, and known limitations—going well beyond the Act's minimums.\",\n\"how\": \"Form a small working group spanning research engineers and domain scientists to draft the schema over two sprints, validate it against three of our active high-risk projects, then publish under an open licence with a community contribution portal.\",\n\"why\": \"Contributing a field-wide documentation standard amplifies the lab's scientific influence, accelerates cross-institutional reproducibility, and demonstrates that rigorous knowledge management is a research asset rather than a compliance burden.\"\n}\n]\n}",
