        "content": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"Article 12 imposes a lifetime technical obligation that cannot be bolted on after deployment: our high-risk systems must emit structured event logs from their first training run, and the scope of those events differs for Annex III biometric systems versus other categories. From an institutional-survival standpoint, the greatest risk is not the regulation itself but the documentation gap that accumulates over years of iterative model updates; if we cannot produce a current, coherent technical dossier on demand during a market-surveillance inspection, we face suspension orders and public enforcement notices that would be catastrophic to our reputation with enterprise clients. On the market side, deployers in healthcare, border control, and biometric verification are already asking whether their vendors meet Article 12, so institutions that treat logging as an afterthought will lose contracts to competitors who built it in from day one. Our response must therefore be threefold: harden our engineering pipeline for automated event capture, institutionalise a documentation-lifecycle process that tracks every system across its operational life, and exploit the resulting infrastructure as a commercial differentiator rather than absorbing it purely as cost.\",\n  \"stance\": 62,\n  \"proposals\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Embed Mandatory Logging Into the Training Pipeline\",\n      \"what\": \"We will integrate automated event-logging modules directly into our model training, validation, and deployment workflows so that every high-risk system we build captures start-and-end timestamps, input references, and operational events from day one.\",\n      \"how\": \"The ML-infrastructure team will add instrumentation hooks to our existing experiment-tracking stack, ensuring each pipeline stage emits structured logs conforming to the Article 12(2) categories before any model is released for external evaluation.\",\n      \"why\": \"Baking compliance into architecture avoids costly retrofits later and protects the lab from enforcement action that could halt deployments or damage our market standing.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Lifetime Technical Documentation Registry\",\n      \"what\": \"We will establish a versioned documentation registry tracking the full technical dossier of each high-risk AI system across its entire operational lifetime, covering algorithms, training-data provenance, risk-management records, and validation outputs as Recital 71 requires.\",\n      \"how\": \"A dedicated compliance-librarian role will own the registry, with automated triggers from our CI/CD pipeline flagging whenever a model update or retraining event mandates a documentation refresh before release sign-off is granted.\",\n      \"why\": \"This keeps us perpetually audit-ready, prevents the documentation gap that most institutions hit at scale, and preserves our reputation as a trustworthy provider to corporate clients.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Productize the Traceability Layer as a Market Feature\",\n      \"what\": \"We will package our Article 12-compliant logging and audit infrastructure into a separable, API-exposed traceability module that downstream deployers can integrate, turning a regulatory obligation into a selling point for regulated-industry clients.\",\n      \"how\": \"The product-engineering group will extract the logging stack built under the first proposal into a standalone SDK with configurable event schemas mapped to Annex III categories, then pilot it with two healthcare and biometric-sector customers this quarter.\",\n      \"why\": \"Converting a compliance cost into a differentiating feature strengthens our revenue base in regulated markets and builds institutional credibility that insulates us from reputational risk during enforcement scrutiny.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
