ger a documentation afterthought but a design constraint that must be satisfied at the build stage. The Annex III point 1(a) specifics for biometric identification systems add granular data-capture requirements (usage timestamps, reference database references, matched input data, verifier identity) that our current pipelines do not natively support. From my compliance vantage, failure to meet these provisions exposes the institution to enforcement under Article 99, potential market withdrawal orders, and reputational harm with both EU public-sector clients and private buyers who will demand conformity attestations. The good news is that the obligation is technical and procedural rather than prohibitive; it does not forbid us from developing any particular class of algorithm or model. Therefore our response should centre on embedding traceability infrastructure into our development lifecycle now, before the August 2026 high-risk deadline forces a reactive scramble.\n\nThat's 5 sentences. Good.\n\n**Number:** 40\n\n**Proposals:**\n\nProposal 1: Engineering/infrastructure\nTitle: \"Embedded Logging Architecture for High-Risk Systems\" (7 words - good)\nWhat: A cross-team engineering project to design and integrate a standardised, tamper-evident event-logging layer into the runtime of every high-risk AI system in our portfolio.\nHow: The platform group will build a shared logging microservice that captures timestamps, input/output metadata, model version, and decision rationale at inference time, with write-once storage and cryptographic chain-of-custody.\nWhy: Satisfies Article 12(1) and (2) as a single architectural component rather than per-project patches, reducing future compliance cost and audit friction.\n\nLet me count words... Title: \"Embedded Logging Architecture for High-Risk Systems\" - that's the title, doesn't count toward word limit? Let me re-read: \"Keep each proposal between 60 and 110 words across its four fields.\" So I think it means what + how + w