nd verified.\",\n\"why\": \"A single reusable component amortises the engineering cost across every current and future project, keeping per-project compliance overhead near zero while preserving iteration speed.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Documentation-Lifecycle Ownership Per High-Risk System\",\n\"what\": \"A standing governance rule that assigns a named documentation steward to every high-risk system at project kickoff, responsible for keeping the technical dossier—algorithms, data provenance, risk-management records—current through each model update until the system is formally decommissioned.\",\n\"how\": \"We extend our existing project-review board with a quarterly compliance checkpoint where stewards present updated dossiers; missed updates trigger an automatic freeze on further deployment or client delivery for that specific system.\",\n\"why\": \"This converts a diffuse institutional obligation into individual accountability, preventing the slow documentation drift that my Compliance Officer correctly identifies as our most likely inspection failure mode.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Repurpose Existing Monitoring Stack for Article 12 Event Capture\",\n\"what\": \"An audit-and-mapping exercise that inventories our current Prometheus metrics, MLflow run logs, and cloud infrastructure event streams to identify which existing data flows already satisfy Article 12(2)(a)–(c), so we patch only the genuine gaps rather than procuring new tooling.\",\n\"how\": \"A two-week engineering audit led by my office cross-references each Article 12 requirement against our live monitoring dashboards; findings become a prioritised gap-list handed to the SDK team as their build specification, avoiding duplicate spend.\",\n\"why\": \"We already pay for and maintain this infrastructure; reusing it keeps the compliance increment within existing operational budgets rather than opening a new line item that our funder will scrutinise at renewal.\"\n}\n]\n}",
