        "content": "{\n\"reasoning\": \"As a researcher whose domain is the knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, and broader scientific impact, I read Article 12 not as an administrative afterthought but as a new architectural constraint that reshapes how we design, test, and validate systems at the laboratory level. The requirement that high-risk AI 'technically allow' automatic event recording over its full lifetime means traceability is no longer optional metadata—it becomes a first-order property of model selection, data-pipeline architecture, and validation methodology from the very first experiment. Recital 71's insistence on comprehensible documentation of algorithms, training processes, and risk management pushes us toward reproducibility-by-design rather than reproducibility as a post-hoc audit exercise. This does not alter which scientific questions we pursue, but it changes how those questions are framed, tested, and reported: our R&D agenda shifts from 'build the model' to 'build the model whose every operational decision remains legible to an auditor over years.' The genuine intellectual opportunity here is that designing for auditability under a fixed information budget is itself an open problem at the intersection of information theory, systems science, and machine learning—one we are well positioned to lead.\",\n\"stance\": 48,\n\"proposals\": [\n{\n\"title\": \"Traceability as a First-Class Architecture Constraint\",\n\"what\": \"We rebuild our model-development pipeline so every experimental system embeds a schema-driven logging layer from day one, treating event-recording capacity as a design variable alongside accuracy and latency.\",\n\"how\": \"A pair of ML researchers plus one systems engineer define a canonical event ontology mapped to Article 12(2)(a)–(c), prototype three logging-integrated architectures on our benchmark suite, and measure the accuracy-overhead tradeoff curve.\",\n\"why\": \"It turns a compliance obligation into an open research question on the measurable cost of auditability, positioning us at the knowledge frontier while producing reusable tooling for every future system.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Open Auditability Benchmark for High-Risk AI\",\n\"what\": \"We develop and publish a public benchmark suite that operationalises Recital 71's call for comprehensible documentation by scoring how well any system's internal state can be reconstructed from its logged events alone, independent of proprietary internals.\",\n\"how\": \"Two research engineers build the evaluation harness around standardised synthetic tasks; we invite three external labs to contribute systems under a shared logging schema; results are released as an open dataset with a reproducibility protocol aligned to Article 12(2).\",\n\"why\": \"It extends our broader scientific impact beyond our own products, establishes a field-level reference point that regulators and competitors must cite, and creates lasting intellectual property for the institution.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Lifetime-Monitoring as Embedded Research Protocol\",\n\"what\": \"We restructure our experimental validation methodology so every high-risk prototype undergoes a minimum six-month continuous-operation period with full Article 12(1) logging active before we declare the research complete, shifting from snapshot evaluation to longitudinal behavioural science.\",\n\"how\": \"Each project's final deliverable now includes a longitudinal log dataset and an anomaly-detection analysis; a dedicated data-steward role at half-time curates these datasets into a searchable internal archive that future projects can query for drift or degradation patterns.\",\n\"why\": \"This embeds scientific rigour directly into our workflow, produces unique long-horizon datasets no competitor possesses, and makes post-market monitoring under Article 72 an extension of research rather than a separate compliance task.\"\n}\n]\n}",
