ther than post-deployment afterthought - changing how we do science, not just engineering.\n\nLet me make sure these are genuinely different:\n- #1 is about architecture/model design (technical/scientific)\n- #2 is about standards/benchmarks (community/field-level impact)\n- #3 is about research methodology/process (internal practice change)\n\nYes, these are distinct enough.\n\nNow let me write the response in 3-5 sentences for part 1, give my score, then the 3 proposals with proper formatting and word counts (60-110 words each across all four fields).\n\nLet me draft:\n\n(1) Reasoning:\nAs a researcher whose domain is the knowledge frontier and intellectual merit, I view Article 12 not as an administrative burden but as a new scientific constraint that reshapes how we design and validate systems. The requirement that high-risk AI \"technically allow\" automatic event recording over its lifetime means traceability is no longer optional metadata—it is an architectural property that should inform model selection, data pipeline design, and validation methodology from the first experiment onward. Recital 71's insistence on comprehensible documentation of algorithms, training processes, and risk management pushes us toward reproducible-by-design research rather than reproducibility as a post-hoc audit exercise. This does not change which scientific questions we pursue, but it changes how those questions are framed, tested, and reported—shifting our R&D agenda from \"build the model\" to \"build the model whose every operational decision is legible to an auditor over years.\" The intellectual opportunity here is real: designing for auditability is itself an open problem at the intersection of information theory, systems science, and machine learning.\n\nThat's 5 sentences. Good.\n\nStance: 48/100\n\nNow proposals - let me count words carefully.\n\nProposal 1:\ntitle: \"Traceability as a First-Class Architecture Constraint\" (7 words) ✓ under 10\nwhat: We restructure our 