 monitoring and gives our systems scientifically defensible documentation.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Lifetime Logs as a Drift-Science Dataset\",\n\"what\": \"We treat the mandatory continuous logs from Article 12(1) as a longitudinal observational dataset on real-world model behaviour, enabling empirical studies of slow degradation, distributional drift, and compounding edge-case encounters that no single benchmark can capture.\",\n\"how\": \"The lab builds an anonymised aggregation pipeline across our deployed systems, then runs time-series analyses and change-point detection to characterise how performance erodes over months and years in the field, publishing findings as open science.\",\n\"why\": \"It turns a regulatory data-retention requirement into a unique empirical resource with broad scientific impact for the AI reliability community.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Mathematical Criteria for Regulatory Modification Thresholds\",\n\"what\": \"We translate the qualitative language of Article 12(2)(a) — situations presenting a risk or constituting a substantial modification — into precise, testable statistical and geometric criteria that can be implemented directly in logging software.\",\n\"how\": \"A theoretical team derives provable sufficient conditions under which logged feature-space shifts cross a modification threshold, validates them against historical incident data from our systems, and packages the results as an open-source detection library for industry use.\",\n\"why\": \"It elevates a compliance checkbox into genuine intellectual work with broader impact, giving the field rigorous tools to answer when an AI system has fundamentally changed.\"\n}\n]\n}",
