        "content": "{\n\"reasoning\": \"As Compliance Officer I read Article 12 not as a filing obligation but as an architectural constraint that must be honoured from the first line of code in any high-risk system we ship. The automatic-logging mandate means our engineering teams need to instrument models during development, not bolt on telemetry after launch, and Recital 71's insistence that technical documentation stay current throughout the lifetime converts what used to be a one-time regulatory dossier into a continuous governance burden. For any of our systems falling under Annex III point 1(a), paragraph 3's four minimum data fields are prescriptive enough that a single missing log entry is a direct non-conformity with measurable legal consequences, so I would prioritise retrofitting our two active high-risk product lines while simultaneously writing logging requirements into the standard operating procedure for every new project entering the pipeline. The net effect is a shift in how we build, test, and document rather than a change to our core research questions.\",\n\"stance\": 62,\n\"proposals\": [\n{\n\"title\": \"Embedded Lifecycle Logging for High-Risk AI Models\",\n\"what\": \"We will design and integrate a standardized, tamper-evident logging layer into the development pipeline of every high-risk system before first deployment. This layer automatically captures events relevant to risk identification, post-market monitoring, and operational oversight per Article 12(2).\",\n\"how\": \"The architecture team will embed instrumentation hooks during training and fine-tuning so that usage timestamps, reference-database pointers, and input-data match records are written immutably at runtime. A Q3 engineering sprint will prototype this for our two highest-risk product lines.\",\n\"why\": \"Baking logging in from inception avoids costly retrofits and signals a design-for-compliance posture to regulators.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Living Technical Documentation Lifecycle Program\",\n\"what\": \"We will establish a governed documentation lifecycle that keeps technical records—algorithms, training-data provenance, risk-management decisions, validation results—current throughout each system's operational life as Recital 71 demands. This is distinct from a one-time compliance file.\",\n\"how\": \"A named documentation owner per product line will trigger updates whenever model weights change, reference databases shift, or post-market incidents occur; quarterly audits will verify completeness against the Article 12 checklist.\",\n\"why\": \"Continuous documentation readiness eliminates last-minute scramble before regulator inquiries and protects our market reputation as a transparent developer.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Annex III Biometric Logging Validation Suite\",\n\"what\": \"We will build a dedicated test harness that verifies whether our biometric identification systems meet the four minimum logging requirements of Article 12(3), including start/end timestamps, reference-database identification, matched input data capture, and verifier person identification.\",\n\"how\": \"The QA team will run staged deployment scenarios in a sandboxed environment, injecting known edge cases such as ambiguous matches or mid-session database updates to confirm every mandated log field populates correctly before production release.\",\n\"why\": \"This targeted validation closes the specific compliance gap where our current systems fall short of paragraph 3's granular recording obligations.\"\n}\n]\n}",
