re matrix by Q3, after which our quarterly budget review formally shifts 8–12% of engineering FTEs toward flagged projects before they hit development milestones.\n- why: Targeted re-allocation prevents the expensive retrofit scenario and keeps unfunded scientific projects on their original timelines without surprise compliance overruns.\n\nLet me count... title: 7, what: ~40, how: ~38, why: ~25. Total roughly 110. Good, right at the edge. Let me trim slightly.\n\n**Proposal 3:**\n- title: Standing Regulatory Engineering Working Group\n- what: A small cross-functional cell (two engineers, one data scientist, one compliance liaison) permanently embedded in the lab to maintain Article 12 technical-documentation templates and run quarterly logging-readiness audits on all prototypes before they advance.\n- how: We allocate four FTEs from existing headcount — no new hires — and give them a protected 20% time block each week, reporting jointly to me and the Compliance Officer so findings feed directly into sprint planning.\n- why: A standing internal capability removes the bottleneck of pulling engineers off research tasks for ad-hoc compliance work, protecting both scientific throughput and regulatory readiness.\n\nLet me count... title: 6, what: ~38, how: ~35, why: ~25. Total roughly 104. Good.\n\nNow let me finalize my reasoning paragraph (3-5 sentences) and stance score, making sure I'm in the Lab Director voice focused on resources, ambition vs. funding, and infrastructure use.\n",
