        "content": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"From a knowledge-frontier standpoint, Article 12 does not alter our core scientific questions but it does reframe the engineering substrate on which we build and evaluate models—automatic lifetime logging becomes an architectural constraint rather than an afterthought, which is itself a novel design problem in systems with complex internal state. The mandate for traceability also creates a new empirical infrastructure: once high-risk systems continuously generate structured event logs over their operational lifetimes, that stream of data opens research avenues in longitudinal model behaviour, drift detection, and post-deployment validation that simply did not exist when evaluation was limited to pre-release snapshots. For our lab's intellectual agenda I see this less as a compliance burden landing on the systems team than as an opportunity to contribute to the emerging methodology of traceable machine learning. The regulatory text is prescriptive about *what* must be logged but silent on *how to design architectures that make logging cheap and complete*, which is precisely the kind of open problem where our research can generate citable, frontier-level results. The broader scientific impact is real: if we establish principled ways to study AI systems in continuous operation rather than as frozen artefacts, the entire field's evaluation practice shifts.\",\n  \"stance\": 45,\n  \"proposals\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Logging-Native Architecture for High-Risk AI\",\n      \"what\": \"A design toolkit that embeds structured event logging directly into model training and inference pipelines so intermediate computation states are automatically captured without degrading throughput.\",\n      \"how\": \"We build an open-source Python layer wrapping PyTorch that hooks forward-pass operations, records tensor provenance to a lightweight append-only store, and exposes a query API; we validate on our biometric-adjacent prototypes first.\",\n      \"why\": \"It advances the frontier by making traceability a first-class architectural property rather than an external compliance bolt-on, which is itself an unsolved problem with broad scientific relevance.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Longitudinal Drift Studies from Operational Logs\",\n      \"what\": \"A programme exploiting mandatory operational log streams to develop new statistical methods for detecting and quantifying model drift and capability degradation in deployed high-risk AI over extended periods.\",\n      \"how\": \"We partner with a regulated credit-scoring deployer to obtain anonymised log archives, then apply change-detection and sequential-testing techniques from time-series analysis to flag deviations beyond calibrated thresholds, publishing open datasets.\",\n      \"why\": \"The mandate inadvertently creates the first large-scale longitudinal window into real-world AI systematics, opening a new empirical domain that pre-deployment snapshot evaluation could never access.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Open Traceability Benchmarks for AI Architectures\",\n      \"what\": \"A community-facing evaluation suite and accompanying publication series that systematically compares different logging and traceability approaches across model architectures, measuring overhead in compute, memory, and latency against completeness of the event record produced.\",\n      \"how\": \"We define a standardised set of ten high-risk task scenarios, instrument open-source reference implementations with candidate logging backends, run controlled experiments varying system scale, and release results as a reproducible benchmark on Hugging Face with full methodological documentation.\",\n      \"why\": \"It serves broader scientific impact by giving the research community a shared empirical yardstick for traceability costs, turning a compliance requirement into a measurable design axis that future architectures must optimise.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}