about process and documentation discipline than changing what we build scientifically\n\nFor the number: I'd say around 35-45. It's not a major overhaul of what we work on (we're still developing AI systems), but it does require meaningful adjustments to how we build, document, and monitor them. The logging requirements add engineering overhead that needs to be factored into project timelines and budgets. But the core scientific direction doesn't change. I'll go with 40 - a moderate adjustment, not business as usual but far from an overhaul.\n\nNow for 3 distinct proposals:\n\n1. Something about integrating logging infrastructure into existing development pipelines (infrastructure/engineering focus)\n2. Something about rethinking project scoping and funding allocation to account for compliance overhead (resource-allocation/funding focus)\n3. Something about leveraging the logging data back into R&D - turning a compliance burden into a research asset (scientific ambition / effective use of infrastructure)\n\nLet me draft these:\n\nProposal 1: Infrastructure/Engineering\nTitle: \"Embed Auto-Logging in Existing ML Pipelines\" \nWhat: Retrofit our current model deployment and monitoring stack with structured event-logging modules that capture the Article 12(3) minimums — session timestamps, reference-database identifiers, matched input records, and verifier IDs. How: Assign two engineers from the platform team to build a lightweight logging layer (event schema plus time-series store) that plugs into our existing Kubernetes-based inference services, reusing the observability tooling we already run for production monitoring. Why: This converts a regulatory obligation into a one-time infrastructure investment that every subsequent system inherits automatically, avoiding per-project compliance costs and keeping our release cadence intact.\n\nLet me count words: Title (8) + What (~45) + How (~35) + Why (~25) = around 113. A bit over. Let me trim.\n\nActually let me recount m