e enthusiastic than the Lab Director (52) because they see scientific value in the data, but not as structurally oriented as the Compliance Officer (60). I'll go with 58 - a moderate adjustment that reorients how we instrument research rather than what questions we ask.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The researcher's concern is knowledge frontier and intellectual merit. Article 12 mandates logging that essentially creates longitudinal behavioral data for AI systems. For a researcher, this is:\n- A new source of empirical data (logs are science)\n- A reproducibility requirement that aligns with good scientific practice\n- A constraint on how we design experiments (we need to log everything relevant)\n- An opportunity: the logging requirements can be designed to serve research questions about system behavior, failure modes, distribution shift\n\nThe researcher would argue: \"Yes, this changes our R&D agenda moderately because we need to build research-grade instrumentation into our systems from day one, and the resulting data streams become a scientific asset.\" But it's not 100 because we're still doing the same fundamental research - just with better data capture.\n\nI'll go with 58. Moderate adjustment. We reorient how we instrument and what questions we can ask, but the core research frontier doesn't shift.\n\nNow for 3 distinct proposals:\n\n1. **Research-grade logging architecture as a scientific instrument** - Design the logging system not just to comply but to capture scientifically meaningful telemetry (distribution shifts, confidence calibration over time, edge-case encounters). This is about turning compliance infrastructure into a research tool.\n\n2. **A public longitudinal benchmark from anonymized operational logs** - Use the mandatory logs (properly anonymized) to create a shared resource for the scientific community studying AI system behavior in deployment. Broader scientific impact.\n\n3. **Pre-registered monitoring protocols tied to Article 12(2)(