ic benchmark for studying AI behaviour under real deployment conditions.\" (40 words)\n- how: \"A dedicated data-stewardship cell applies differential-privacy transforms to the raw logs, publishes versioned snapshots quarterly with full provenance metadata, and maintains an accompanying methodological paper describing coverage gaps and selection effects so downstream researchers can calibrate their inferences.\" (39 words)\n- why: \"It positions our lab at the centre of a new empirical literature on deployed-AI reliability while discharging traceability obligations once rather than repeatedly to each regulator.\" (26 words)\n\nTotal: 8 + 40 + 39 + 26 = 113. Slightly over. Let me trim.\n\nLet me revise:\n- what: \"Create a k-anonymised release of operational log streams—match events, reference-database versions, verifier actions per Article 12(3)—as a public benchmark for studying AI behaviour in real deployment.\" (30 words)\n- how: \"A data-stewardship cell applies differential-privacy transforms, publishes versioned quarterly snapshots with provenance metadata, and documents coverage gaps so downstream researchers can calibrate their inferences.\" (26 words)\n- why: \"It positions our lab at the centre of a new empirical literature on deployed-AI reliability while discharging traceability obligations once rather than repeatedly per regulator.\" (25 words)\n\nTotal: 8 + 30 + 26 + 25 = 89. Good.\n\nProposal 3:\n- title: \"Pre-Registered Drift Hypotheses Embedded in Logging Protocols\" \n- what: \"Treat each Article 12(2)(a)-(c) logging category as a pre-registered hypothesis about when and how our systems will exhibit risk-relevant drift, substantial modification, or performance degradation, turning compliance monitoring into structured experimental inquiry.\" (35 words)\n- how: \"Before any system enters the EU market, the responsible researcher files a one-page protocol specifying the null hypothesis, the log-derived statistic that would falsify it, and the decision threshold for triggering investigation; the logging pipeline is then configured to compute exactly those statistics in real time.\" (46 words)\n- why: \"This makes the knowledge frontier our compliance mechanism: every regulatory trigger becomes a published, replicable finding rather than an opaque internal alert.\" (21 words)\n\nTotal: 7 + 35 + 46 + 21 = 109. Good, within range.\n\nNow let me write the reasoning in 3-5 sentences from the researcher's perspective focused on knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, and broader scientific impact.\n\nLet me draft:\n\"Article 12 is, for a research institution, less a constraint than an underappreciated instrument: mandatory longitudinal logging of high-risk system behaviour is precisely the kind of structured empirical data that has been missing from our interpretability and robustness literature. The intellectual merit of our work depends on being able to say not just 'the model works in the lab' but 'here is what it did across 40,000 deployment hours under distribution shift,' and Article 12(2) gives us a regulatory spine around which to build exactly that evidence base. I would push back on framing this as pure overhead; the knowledge frontier advances when our compliance datastreams are designed with the same rigour we apply to experimental controls. At the same time, I am not asking for a pivot in research questions—the systems we study and the hypotheses we test remain the same—but for a re-architecture of how we instrument them so that every mandatory log is simultaneously a scientific observation.\"\n\nThat's 5 sentences. Good.\n\nStance: 58/100. Moderate adjustment - we change how we build and instrument, not what we ask.\n\nLet me finalize everything.\n",
