        "role": "assistant",
        "content": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"Article 12 turns logging from a nice-to-have observability feature into a first-class technical requirement that must be designed in before prototyping begins, not stitched on after a model works. For my team this is actually manageable if we treat structured event emission as part of the standard architecture template: every prototype inherits a typed emitter and a versioned schema, so compliance becomes zero marginal cost per new experiment. The real friction point is the 'lifetime of the system' language in paragraph one combined with Annex III 1(a)'s granular field list—our prototypes iterate fast, which means event schemas will drift across model versions, and we need migration tooling to keep historical logs queryable for auditors years from now. I would not restructure what we build; I would restructure how every build is instrumented, starting this sprint so the next three high-risk deliverables are audit-ready out of the box.\",\n  \"stance\": 40,\n  \"proposals\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Embed Event-Schema Logging in Prototyping Stack\",\n      \"what\": \"A shared infrastructure layer that every new prototype emits structured events through a typed event bus, with schema definitions versioned alongside the model code. This makes log compliance an architectural default rather than a post-hoc audit.\",\n      \"how\": \"We extend our existing CI/CD pipeline to include a lint step that validates every service emits required event types before merge; prototypes inherit the emitter from a base class so developers never hand-write logging calls.\",\n      \"why\": \"It protects prototyping velocity because engineers stop context-switching between model logic and compliance plumbing, and it makes debugging faster since structured events replace ad-hoc print statements.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Biometric Logging Reference Implementation and SDK\",\n      \"what\": \"A reusable code template satisfying all four sub-requirements of Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems, capturing session timestamps, reference-database pointers, matched inputs, and verifier identities as a forkable starting point.\",\n      \"how\": \"Two engineers spend one sprint extracting logging hooks from our current prototype into a parameterised SDK with configurable retention; we publish it on the internal model registry with unit tests asserting each field is present and typed.\",\n      \"why\": \"It eliminates the highest-friction compliance task from every future biometric project so teams ship prototypes weeks earlier rather than days spent guessing what the regulator expects in logs.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Lifetime Log Schema Migration and Validation\",\n      \"what\": \"A tooling layer that validates historical logs against evolving event schemas across system versions, ensuring a log written under version one remains queryable after the model is substantially modified years later. It handles forward and backward compatibility automatically.\",\n      \"how\": \"We build a CLI plus CI hook running schema-diff checks on every event-definition change; it auto-generates migration scripts for stored logs and flags any field whose removal would break Article 12(3) minimums.\",\n      \"why\": \"The lifetime-of-system language means our logging contract cannot be broken by refactors, so this keeps long-running deployments audit-ready without manual data-wrangling.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
