shes, and verifier identities are recorded uniformly across all systems.\nHow (~30): Platform engineering designs a plug-in SDK by Q3, then retrofits it into each active high-risk project over two sprints, testing against Annex III 1(a) minimum fields before shipment.\nWhy (~25): One reusable component cuts per-project compliance cost and guarantees no deployed system lacks the automatic-recording obligation that Article 12(1) makes non-negotiable.\n\nTotal: 7 + 40 + 30 + 25 = 102. Good, within range.\n\n**Proposal 2:**\n- title (8): \"Dedicated Post-Market Surveillance Unit for Deployed AI\"\n- what (~38): A small standing team embedded in operations whose mandate is to consume the Article 12 logs from every live high-risk system and flag events that could indicate a risk under Article 79(1) or a substantial modification. They own the escalation path to engineering and to regulators.\n- how (~32): We hire two data analysts and one regulatory liaison in Q4, wire them into the logging middleware's alerting pipeline, and give them authority to pause a deployment pending review.\n- why (~27): Having an internal watchdog converts passive log storage into active risk detection, protecting the lab from both enforcement action and reputational damage when something goes wrong post-launch.\n\nTotal: 8 + 38 + 32 + 27 = 105. Good.\n\n**Proposal 3:**\n- title (9): \"Compliance Documentation Standard Baked into Every Project Charter\"\n- what (~40): A mandatory internal standard requiring that every high-risk project charter includes a living technical-documentation section covering general characteristics, algorithms, training data, testing, and the risk-management system as Recital 71 demands. The document is updated at each release cycle.\n- how (~32): I will draft the template with legal counsel by end of Q2, train all project leads in a half-day workshop, and gate any code merge on documentation currency checks automated into our CI pipeline.\n- why (~25): Keeping technica