dy being retrofitted for compliance; define a drift-metric taxonomy in the first quarter, then run quarterly analyses against the accumulated log corpus, publishing results in AI-reliability venues once systems have at least twelve months of operational data.\",\n\"why\": \"It converts a regulatory obligation into a unique dataset no competitor or academic group has access to, positioning the lab as the reference point for empirical AI-lifetime-stability research and strengthening our broader scientific impact on the reliability frontier.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Log-Native Experimental Design Protocol\",\n\"what\": \"Adopt a formal 'log-first' methodology in which every new experiment or model-validation study is designed around the structured event fields that Article 12(2) and (3) will ultimately require in deployment, so that research-phase data schemas are compatible with production logging from day one. This eliminates the retrofit gap between lab notebooks and operational traceability.\",\n\"how\": \"The methods group drafts a one-page template specifying mandatory fields (input provenance, reference-database version hash, model checkpoint ID, evaluation metric snapshots, human-in-the-loop verification identity) for every experimental run; CI pipelines are updated to reject any experiment submission that lacks those fields, and the template is reviewed annually against evolving Annex III guidance.\",\n\"why\": \"It raises the intellectual merit of our internal research by enforcing structured provenance on every result, making findings immediately reproducible by colleagues and auditable without a separate documentation pass, while keeping production readiness continuous rather than a pre-launch scramble.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Open Technical-Documentation Repository for Published Systems\",\n\"what\": \"Create a publicly accessible repository where, for each high-risk system we publish research on, the Recital 71 technical documentation—algorithm description, 