eed is preserved while Article 12(1)'s 'technically allow' requirement is met by construction rather than retrofit.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Mandatory Logging-Architecture Gate in Sprint One\",\n\"what\": \"A structural change to sprint cadence where the first week of every high-risk project designs the event-recording schema, maps which inference steps must be logged per Article 12(2), and commits a living documentation template to version control.\",\n\"how\": \"Lead engineers own the logging-design deliverable as a formal work item; the documentation template auto-updates via CI hooks on model checkpoint commits, enforcing Recital 71's living-document requirement mechanically.\",\n\"why\": \"It prevents the most costly failure mode in prototyping—discovering at integration that a critical event path was never instrumented—and keeps technical documentation current without adding separate documentation headcount.\"\n},\n{\n\"title\": \"Annex III Biometric-ID Capture Module\",\n\"what\": \"A domain-specific engineering component for biometric identification prototypes that records the four mandated data points—session start and end timestamps, reference-database identity hash, matched input records, and verifier personnel IDs—as a structured audit record per use event.\",\n\"how\": \"The module intercepts our matching pipeline, serializing the four mandated fields to an append-only tamper-evident store with a query interface for Article 72 post-market monitoring.\",\n\"why\": \"It converts Annex III point 1(a)'s specific capture obligations from an open-ended compliance risk into a bounded engineering task that can be prototyped, tested, and shipped independently of the core recognition algorithm.\"\n}\n]\n}",
