cle 12(3), then embeds it as a mandatory component in our system-design checklist so no project passes architecture review without it. \n- why: Eliminates the risk of shipping a high-risk system that technically cannot auto-log, which would be an immediate compliance failure and market-access barrier. \nTotal: 6 + 38 + 42 + 25 = 111 words. Slightly over. Let me trim.\n\nLet me redo:\n- title (6): Build a Unified Event-Logging Substrate\n- what (30): A shared middleware layer that every high-risk system integrates with to capture automatic event logs across its full operational lifetime, satisfying Article 12(1) before deployment.\n- how (38): Platform engineering designs a standardized logging API and schema covering the Article 12(3) minimum fields, then embeds it as a mandatory item in our architecture-review checklist so no project advances without it.\n- why (22): Prevents shipping any high-risk system that technically cannot auto-log, which would be an immediate compliance failure blocking market access.\n\nTotal: 6 + 30 + 38 + 22 = 96 words. Good, within 60-110.\n\n**Proposal 2: Lifetime Technical-Documentation Pipeline (4 words)**\n- what: A governed document-lifecycle system that keeps each high-risk system's technical file—algorithms, training data provenance, risk-management records, validation reports—continuously updated and audit-ready over the system's entire life, as Recital 71 requires. \n- how: We assign a Documentation Owner to every product line who maintains a living technical dossier in our compliance repository, with automated version triggers tied to each code release, model retraining event, or substantial modification flagged by our logging substrate. \n- why: A current, comprehensive technical file is what regulators will demand first in any conformity assessment, and its absence is the fastest route to an enforcement action that damages reputation. \nTotal: 4 + 38 + 45 + 35 = 122 words. Too long. Let me trim.\n\n- title (4): Lifetime Technical-Documentation Pipeline\n- what (30): A governed system keeping each high-risk project's technical file—algorithms, training data, risk records, validation reports—continuously updated and audit-ready over the system's full life per Recital 71.\n- how (35): Each product line gets a Documentation Owner maintaining a living dossier in our compliance repository, with version triggers tied to code releases, retraining events, or substantial modifications flagged by logging.\n- why (28): Regulators will demand this file first in any conformity assessment; its absence is the fastest route to enforcement and reputational damage.\n\nTotal: 4 + 30 + 35 + 28 = 97 words. Good.\n\n**Proposal 3: Pre-Market Traceability Audit Sprint (5 words)**\n- what: A quarterly internal audit exercise where compliance engineers stress-test each high-risk system's logging and traceability against Article 12(2) criteria—risk-identification, post-market monitoring support, and operational oversight—before external review. \n- how: A rotating team of two engineers and one compliance analyst runs scripted scenario tests on the logging substrate (e.g., \"simulate a risk event under Article 79(1); can you trace it end-to-end?\"), documenting gaps in a shared remediation tracker. \n- why: Catching traceability gaps internally, before a notified body or market surveillance authority finds them, protects both our timeline and our credibility with clients and regulators. \nTotal: 5 + 38 + 40 + 30 = 113 words. Slightly over. Trim.\n\n- title (5): Pre-Market Traceability Audit Sprint\n- what (32): A quarterly internal audit where compliance engineers stress-test each high-risk system's logging against Article 12(2) criteria—risk identification, post-market monitoring support, operational oversight—before external review.\n- how (36): Rotating teams of two engineers and one analyst run scripted scenario tests on the logging substrate, asking \"can you trace this event end-to-end?\", and log gaps in a s