t - pivot or restructure product portfolio to ensure all high-risk systems meet Annex III(1)(a) specific logging minimums, or develop a compliance-as-a-service offering\n\nActually, let me think more carefully:\n- One is about the engineering/technical build (the \"what\" of logging)\n- One is about internal process/governance (who owns it, how docs stay current)\n- One is about product strategy/market (how this changes what we build and sell)\n\nLet me draft these:\n\n**Reasoning (3-5 sentences):**\nArticle 12 mandates that high-risk AI systems technically allow for automatic event logging over their entire lifetime, with specific minimums for Annex III(1)(a) biometric identification systems. Recital 71 reinforces that technical documentation must be kept current throughout the system's lifetime and must cover algorithms, training data, testing processes, and risk management. For our lab, this is not a one-time checkbox but a continuous operational obligation embedded in every high-risk system we develop or maintain. We need to treat logging as a core architectural requirement rather than an afterthought, ensure our documentation lifecycle is maintained post-deployment, and verify that our Annex III(1)(a) products meet the four specific data points (usage periods, reference databases, matched input data, verifier identities). Failure to comply risks enforcement action, market withdrawal orders, and reputational damage that would be existential for an R&D institution whose credibility depends on regulatory trust.\n\n**Stance number:** I'd say around 55-60. It's not a complete overhaul (we're not stopping what we do), but it requires meaningful integration of compliance engineering into our R&D process, ongoing documentation maintenance, and potentially adjusting product priorities. Not 100 because the core research doesn't change fundamentally - but not 30 either because logging architecture, documentation lifecycle, and Annex III(1)(a) specifics require real struct