umulated logs for anomalies, correlation patterns, and risk-precursor signals.\",\n      \"why\": \"This converts a regulatory cost into a unique longitudinal corpus that advances the lab's standing at the frontier of trustworthy-AI empirical science.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Formal Criteria for Substantial Modification in Iterative ML\",\n      \"what\": \"Article 12(2)(a) obliges logging when a system undergoes substantial modification, yet the Act leaves that boundary undefined; the lab can pioneer formal criteria and detection algorithms delineating where iterative model updates cross into that territory.\",\n      \"how\": \"Build on sensitivity-analysis and distributional-shift literature to propose a tiered taxonomy of modification magnitude, validate it against emerging regulatory guidance, and publish as an open preprint before finalising internal policy.\",\n      \"why\": \"This positions the lab at the intersection of ML theory and regulatory science, generating work with both citation impact and standard-setting influence.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Living Documentation Framework for Reproducible AI Pipelines\",\n      \"what\": \"Recital 71 demands documentation of algorithms, data, training, testing, and validation kept current across the system lifetime; the lab can build an open-source toolchain auto-generating this living documentation directly from experiment trackers and pipelines.\",\n      \"how\": \"Integrate metadata-extraction hooks into our MLOps stack so every retraining run or dataset change automatically updates a structured record, then release the framework to the research community as a reproducibility standard.\",\n      \"why\": \"This raises the intellectual rigour of all lab output by making provenance transparent by default, while producing a citable open contribution that extends our scientific footprint beyond internal products.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
