        "content": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"As Compliance Officer, I see Article 12 as fundamentally an architecture mandate rather than a paperwork obligation: the system must technically allow automatic recording of events over its entire lifetime, which means our R&D teams need to treat log-generation as a first-class design requirement from the outset. The specific minima in paragraph 3 for Annex III point 1(a) systems—timestamps per use, reference-database identifiers, matched input data, and verifier identities—will directly shape how we engineer those particular modules. Recital 71 extends this into an ongoing lifetime obligation: technical documentation must remain current through retraining, modification, and decommissioning, so our QA and record-keeping processes require structural upgrades beyond a one-time file compilation. I do not see this as altering which product areas or research questions we pursue; rather it changes how we build, document, and surveil every high-risk system we ship. The commercial stakes are unambiguous: failure to demonstrate traceability exposes us to market-withdrawal orders and reputational damage that no volume of R&D output can offset.\",\n  \"stance\": 35,\n  \"proposals\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Embed Automatic Logging into System Architecture\",\n      \"what\": \"The lab will integrate event-logging modules directly into the core codebase of every high-risk AI system during the design phase, ensuring that start and end timestamps, reference-database identifiers, matched input data, and verifier identities are captured automatically without manual intervention.\",\n      \"how\": \"We will mandate a logging interface specification in our internal engineering standards and require architecture-review sign-off confirming log coverage against Article 12(2) and (3) before any system exits the development stage.\",\n      \"why\": \"Building logs into the architecture from inception prevents costly retrofitting and demonstrates to regulators that traceability is a design principle rather than an afterthought, protecting our market reputation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Lifetime Technical Documentation Lifecycle Program\",\n      \"what\": \"We will establish a governed process that keeps the technical documentation for each high-risk system current throughout its operational life, covering algorithms, training data, testing results, risk-management records, and any substantial modifications as Recital 71 requires.\",\n      \"how\": \"A designated compliance liaison per project will maintain a living documentation repository with version control, triggered updates on every model retraining or parameter change, and quarterly completeness audits against the Regulation's requirements.\",\n      \"why\": \"Regulators expect documentation that mirrors the system's actual state at any point in time; a systematic lifecycle process shields us from the reputational damage of producing stale files during an inspection.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Post-Market Log Analytics and Anomaly Detection\",\n      \"what\": \"The lab will build an internal analytics pipeline that continuously ingests the operational logs mandated by Article 12(2) to detect patterns indicating emerging risks, substantial modifications, or deviations relevant to post-market monitoring under Article 72.\",\n      \"how\": \"We will deploy a dedicated data-engineering team to construct query tools and threshold-based alerting on log streams, with weekly review sessions where compliance staff assess flagged events for escalation or corrective action.\",\n      \"why\": \"Turning raw logs into actionable surveillance gives us early warning of non-conformance before regulators do, preserving both our compliance standing and the trust our clients place in our systems.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
