        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to respond as a Development Engineer at an R&D institution that develops AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain concern is technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nLet me analyze Article 12 (Record-keeping) from my perspective:\n\nThe regulation requires high-risk AI systems to technically allow for automatic recording of events (logs) over their lifetime. For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification, etc.), there are specific minimum logging requirements including start/end times, reference databases, input data matches, and verification personnel IDs.\n\nFrom my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and prototyping speed/quality:\n\n(1) How should the lab respond?\n- The core challenge is that automatic logging over system lifetime adds architectural complexity to every high-risk AI system we build. This affects prototyping because you can't just throw away prototypes - they need to have logging infrastructure from day one or retrofit it later (which is expensive and error-prone).\n- The \"lifetime\" requirement means our systems need durable, tamper-evident log storage that persists beyond any single deployment cycle.\n- For Annex III 1(a) systems, the specific requirements (reference database provenance, input data matching records, verification personnel IDs) create coupling between the ML pipeline and operational/audit infrastructure.\n- From a prototyping speed perspective, this means we need standardized logging SDKs/libraries that can be dropped into new prototypes quickly, rather than building custom logging per project.\n- The \"technically allow\" phrasing gives some flexibility in implementation but not in capability - the system must be technically capable of these recordings.\n\n(2) Stance number: I'd say around 45-55. It's a moderate adjustment. We don't need to overhaul what we work on (we're still building AI systems),