g infrastructure, potentially redesign system architectures, and allocate staff to compliance documentation. I'd say around 35-45. Let me go with 40 — a moderate adjustment to how we build and deploy, without changing the core research agenda.\n\nNow for 3 distinct proposals:\n\n1. One should be about engineering/infrastructure — building logging architecture into existing systems (infrastructure investment)\n2. One should be about process/workflow — restructuring the R&D pipeline to embed compliance from design phase (process change)\n3. One should be about a specific technical capability or research direction that's now necessitated or accelerated by this requirement (e.g., developing a lightweight logging framework that itself becomes an IP asset, or repurposing existing monitoring infrastructure)\n\nLet me make them genuinely different:\n- Proposal 1: Infrastructure — retrofit/build logging middleware into current high-risk system deployments\n- Proposal 2: Process — restructure the development lifecycle to embed Article 12 compliance as a gate in the R&D pipeline (not an afterthought)\n- Proposal 3: Research/IP pivot — develop and open-source (or license) a domain-specific logging framework that could become a product or competitive differentiator\n\nLet me draft these carefully, keeping word counts between 60-110 words per proposal.\n\nLet me count words carefully for each proposal.\n\nProposal 1:\nTitle: \"Retrofit Logging Middleware Across Active Deployments\" \nWhat: We would build a centralized logging middleware layer that injects automatic event capture into our three currently deployed high-risk systems without rewriting their core inference pipelines. This is an engineering retrofit project targeting the specific data points Article 12(3) mandates for Annex III category 1(a) systems.\nHow: A small team of four engineers, working over two sprints per system, would wrap each model's input/output interfaces with standardized log-emitting adapters that write to our existing time-series database infrastructure. We already maintain these databases for operational monitoring; the work is integration, not new procurement.\nWhy: This converts a compliance obligation into an incremental use of infrastructure we have already funded, protecting existing grants from being diverted to build logging systems from scratch.\n\nLet me count: \"We would build a centralized logging middleware layer that injects automatic event capture into our three currently deployed high-risk systems without rewriting their core inference pipelines.\" (27) \"This is an engineering retrofit project targeting the specific data points Article 12(3) mandates for Annex III category 1(a) systems.\" (19) = 46 what\n\n\"A small team of four engineers, working over two sprints per system, would wrap each model's input/output interfaces with standardized log-emitting adapters that write to our existing time-series database infrastructure.\" (32) \"We already maintain these databases for operational monitoring; the work is integration, not new procurement.\" (15) = 47 how\n\n\"This converts a compliance obligation into an incremental use of infrastructure we have already funded, protecting existing grants from being diverted to build logging systems from scratch.\" (28) why\n\nTotal: 7 + 46 + 47 + 28 = 128. Too long. Need to trim to 110 max.\n\nLet me redo:\n\nTitle: \"Retrofit Logging Middleware Across Active Deployments\" (7 words)\n\nWhat: We would build a centralized logging middleware layer that injects automatic event capture into our three deployed high-risk systems without rewriting core inference pipelines, targeting the specific data points Article 12(3) mandates. \nHow: A four-engineer team working over two sprints per system would wrap each model's input/output interfaces with log-emitting adapters writing to our existing time-series databases already used for operational monitoring; this is integration, not new procurement. \nWhy: This converts a