ead line, roughly fifteen percent of total FTE-months, dedicated to logging integration and lifetime maintenance planning.\",\n      \"how\": \"The quarterly portfolio review scores proposals on scientific merit and on how efficiently they reuse the shared logging platform; projects needing heavy customisation face a higher cost threshold for approval.\",\n      \"why\": \"Making compliance cost explicit in every budget line prevents quiet attrition of research capacity across the lab.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Compliance-by-Design Gate at Architecture Review\",\n      \"what\": \"A mandatory checkpoint placed at the system-architecture milestone where a designated engineer verifies that logging architecture satisfies Article 12(1) through (2) before model-training compute is allocated.\",\n      \"how\": \"We add a two-hour session to our existing monthly architecture board; the check covers event-recording hooks, which Annex III point 1(a) fields apply to the system, and whether the log schema supports Article 72 post-market monitoring queries.\",\n      \"why\": \"Catching gaps before expensive training runs avoids re-architecting validated pipelines retroactively for traceability.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
