        "content": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"As a Development Engineer, my immediate read is that Article 12(1)'s requirement to 'technically allow for the automatic recording of events' is an architectural constraint on our codebase topology and deployment pipeline, not a redirection of which AI systems we build. The practical engineering question is whether we can deliver one shared event-recording layer that every high-risk prototype imports at scaffold time, so compliance logging carries near-zero marginal cost per project and does not tax iteration speed. The Annex III 1(a) minimum fields — session start/end timestamps, reference database version hashes, matched input records, verifier identifiers — are all deterministic metadata captures: straightforward to instrument if the architecture anticipates them from sprint one, painful and slow to retrofit once a model sits inside a deployed inference pipeline. My concern is that without a unified SDK and an automated CI gate, eight concurrent project teams will each invent ad-hoc logging schemas, producing seven incompatible log formats that make our prototypes harder to debug rather than easier. Done once as shared infrastructure with a hard pipeline check, the regulation actually pushes us toward better engineering hygiene and faster prototyping downstream.\",\n  \"stance\": 37,\n  \"proposals\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Shared Structured Event-Recording SDK\",\n      \"what\": \"A single importable library that every high-risk system prototype links at initialization, automatically capturing the Article 12(3) fields — session start and end timestamps, reference database version hashes, matched input records, and verifier session IDs — into a normalized event stream without per-project instrumentation work.\",\n      \"how\": \"Two senior engineers build the SDK against our existing telemetry stack over six weeks; it exposes a thin decorator API so model-inference wrappers emit structured events with zero additional code in research notebooks or service handlers.\",\n      \"why\": \"Eliminates the per-project logging engineering burden that would otherwise consume two to three sprints of prototype time and produces one consistent audit schema across all deliverables.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Compliance-Gated CI/CD Logging Stage\",\n      \"what\": \"A mandatory pipeline stage in our deployment workflow that fails any high-risk system build if the event-recording SDK is not linked, required schema fields are missing from the manifest declaration, or log-retention configuration falls below the lifetime threshold.\",\n      \"how\": \"We add a static-analysis and integration-test gate to our existing GitLab CI runners; the check parses the build manifest for the logging dependency and executes a smoke test that runs one full inference cycle while asserting event emission on every required field.\",\n      \"why\": \"Catches missing instrumentation at commit time rather than at client-audit time, protecting prototype throughput by preventing weeks of retrofit work on already-deployed models.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Internal Log-Replay Debugging Console\",\n      \"what\": \"A developer-facing tool that ingests the lifetime event stream from any deployed high-risk prototype and replays individual inference sessions in a sandboxed environment, letting engineers inspect exactly which reference database version was queried, which inputs matched, and when verifier overrides occurred.\",\n      \"how\": \"We extend our existing Jupyter-based debugging notebooks with an event-stream connector module; the console pulls logs from the shared middleware's storage layer and renders them as step-through timelines tied to the model weights active at inference time.\",\n      \"why\": \"Converts what would be a pure compliance overhead into a first-class debugging asset that shortens prototyping iteration cycles by making failure reproduction deterministic and tracea