ric use-case without re-deriving the schema. \nWhy: It compresses time-to-first-compliant-prototype for Annex III(1a) systems from weeks of architecture discussion to hours, preserving our speed advantage while meeting the regulation's floor. \nTotal: 8 + 38 + 36 + 29 = 111. One over. Let me trim \"the\" somewhere or shorten.\n\nWhy: It compresses time-to-first-compliant-prototype for Annex III(1a) systems from weeks of architecture talk to hours, preserving our speed edge while meeting the regulation's floor. \nTotal: 8 + 38 + 36 + 28 = 110. Good.\n\nProposal 3: \"Automated Log-Completeness Gates in CI/CD\"\nTitle: Automated log-completeness gates in every CI pipeline (9) - count: Automated log-completeness gates in every CI pipeline = 8 ✓\nWhat: A set of CI checks that, on each prototype push, verify the running system actually emits all required event types and fields for its declared risk class before the build is marked green.\nHow: We write small harness scripts per Annex III category that spin up the model stub, feed synthetic inputs, and assert on the log stream for presence of start/end timestamps, database references, or verifier IDs as applicable; failures block merge.\nWhy: It shifts compliance verification left into the daily dev loop so gaps are caught in minutes rather than discovered by auditors months later.\n\nCount:\nTitle: Automated log-completeness gates in every CI pipeline = 8\nWhat: A set of CI checks that, on each prototype push, verify the running system actually emits all required event types and fields for its declared risk class before the build is marked green. \nHow: We write small harness scripts per Annex III category that spin up the model stub, feed synthetic inputs, and assert on the log stream for start/end timestamps, database references, or verifier IDs; failures block merge. \nWhy: It shifts compliance verification left into the daily dev loop so gaps surface in minutes rather than at audit time months later. \nTotal: 8 + 38 +