ed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Pre-Development Logging-Schema Gate in Project Intake\",\n      \"what\": \"A mandatory checkpoint at project kickoff where every proposed high-risk AI system must submit a logging specification—defining which events will be captured, their granularity, and retention period—before engineering resources are allocated.\",\n      \"how\": \"The research operations office adds this as the second gate in our existing stage-gate funding workflow; the spec is reviewed by a small cross-functional panel including one engineer, one compliance officer, and me to confirm it satisfies Article 12(2)(a) through (c).\",\n      \"why\": \"Front-loading the logging design prevents costly mid-project rearchitecture when a system that was not built with automatic event capture in mind later must be retrofitted before deployment.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Log-Volume Economics Study for Lifetime Retention\",\n      \"what\": \"A six-month internal research project to model the storage and compute costs of continuous event logging across a system's full operational lifetime, identifying where data compression or tiered retention can reduce expenditure without violating Article 12.\",\n      \"how\": \"We assign two graduate researchers from our systems-efficiency group to instrument three pilot deployments with the new middleware, collect real throughput data over eight weeks, and build a cost-per-year forecast scaled to our fleet of twenty active high-risk prototypes.\",\n      \"why\": \"Because open-ended lifetime retention could strain our fixed R&D budget if we default to naive full-fidelity storage, this study gives me defensible numbers for negotiating tiered retention contracts with the IT department.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
