        "content": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"Article 12's opening phrase—systems \\\"shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events\\\"—is an architecture constraint that lands directly on our prototyping framework rather than on a documentation team somewhere else; if our scaffold does not natively emit a structured event stream, no amount of post-hoc scripting closes the gap without touching every layer after the model already works. The good news from an engineering-velocity standpoint is that logging infrastructure (typed events, append-only stores, schema validation) is mature solved technology, so the risk here is integration speed and overhead budgeting, not fundamental feasibility. What does slow me down is the Annex III 1(a) minimum-fields list: session timestamps, reference-database identifiers, matched-input payloads, and verifier identities force structured I/O at our biometric pipeline's API boundary, which means any prototype that treats those values as opaque blobs will fail a compliance architecture review before it reaches production. The genuine engineering question I need answered early is the latency-and-memory delta that mandatory structured emission adds per inference call, because if that overhead pushes a candidate past its real-time SLO we have to choose between model quality and regulatory fitness at design time, not after six months of build-out. Net effect: our prototyping toolchain and CI gates need restructuring now, but the scientific direction of what models and architectures we explore is largely untouched.\",\n  \"stance\": 43,\n  \"proposals\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Compliance-Native Prototype Scaffold\",\n      \"what\": \"A templated code-generation tool that stamps every new high-risk system prototype with pre-wired event-recording hooks, structured log emitters, and the Annex III 1(a) minimum fields already populated as typed data-class stubs.\",\n      \"how\": \"The platform team extracts our existing experiment-tracking decorators into a shared Python and TypeScript package; CI lints every new repository to confirm the scaffold is present before merge, so engineers never hand-roll logging after the model works.\",\n      \"why\": \"Cuts the integration lead-time for compliance logging from days to zero at prototype inception, preserving our iteration velocity without deferring a hard regulatory deadline.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Single Event Bus for Research and Compliance\",\n      \"what\": \"Replace the parallel stacks of internal MLflow-style tracking plus an ad-hoc deployment log with one append-only event store whose schema satisfies Article 12 fields natively while remaining queryable for ablation analysis.\",\n      \"how\": \"Engineering rewrites our deploy pipeline to publish every inference session, training epoch, and validation run as typed events onto a Kafka topic; downstream consumers—research dashboards, compliance archive, post-market monitor—subscribe independently.\",\n      \"why\": \"One infrastructure investment serves three regulatory obligations and internal experimentation simultaneously, avoiding the double-maintenance cost of two logging systems that drift out of sync over a multi-year product lifetime.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Logging-Overhead Viability Benchmark Suite\",\n      \"what\": \"A reproducible micro-benchmark harness that measures the wall-clock latency and memory-footprint delta introduced by Article 12-compliant log emission across our target model architectures, including vision encoders and biometric classifiers.\",\n      \"how\": \"The benchmark runs each candidate architecture with logging enabled versus disabled over a fixed synthetic workload; results gate architecture-selection meetings so we never commit multi-month build cycles to a design whose compliance overhead breaches a pre-agreed SLO threshold.\",\n      \"why\": \"Gives the team an early quantitative signal on technical viability bef