in and design patterns, but doesn't fundamentally change what research questions we pursue.\n\nLet me settle on around 45. The technical viability is clear (logging is well-understood engineering), the impact on speed/quality of prototyping is moderate (we need to build logging into our framework layer, which takes sprint-time but isn't a rewrite). It's not a major overhaul because logging infrastructure is mature technology; it IS a real adjustment because we need to restructure how our prototypes emit events and ensure our CI/CD pipelines capture the right fields.\n\nNow for 3 distinct proposals:\n\n1. Something about prototyping framework/tooling - building a \"compliance-aware\" prototype scaffold that auto-generates logging hooks. This is about SPEED of prototyping while meeting compliance.\n\n2. Something about the technical architecture pattern - designing a unified event-bus/telemetry layer that serves both our internal experimentation tracking AND the Article 12 requirements simultaneously, so we don't maintain two stacks.\n\n3. Something about a specific prototype validation exercise or benchmark - creating a \"log-fidelity\" test harness that validates whether a given model architecture can emit the required structured events without degrading inference performance by more than X%. This is about technical viability assessment.\n\nLet me make sure these are genuinely different:\n- Proposal 1: Developer tooling (scaffold/templates for new prototypes)\n- Proposal 2: Infrastructure (unified event bus serving dual purpose)\n- Proposal 3: Quality gate/benchmark (performance-overhead validation of logging)\n\nThese are distinct: one is a dev-experience improvement, one is an infrastructure decision, one is a testing/quality mechanism.\n\nLet me now write the response in proper format. Each proposal must be between 60-110 words across all four fields combined. Let me count carefully.\n\nProposal 1 - \"Compliance-native prototype scaffold\"\n- title: Compliance-Na