h candidate architecture with logging enabled and disabled over a fixed synthetic workload; results gate architecture-selection meetings so we never ship a design whose compliance overhead exceeds a pre-agreed SLO threshold.\n- why: Gives the team an early, quantitative signal on technical viability before committing multi-month build cycles to a model whose logging cost would make it unviable in production.\n\nLet me count words for each proposal carefully (title + what + how + how combined):\n\nProposal 1:\n- title: \"Compliance-Native Prototype Scaffold\" = 3 words\n- what: \"A templated code-generation tool that stamps every new high-risk system prototype with pre-wired event-recording hooks, structured log emitters, and the Annex III 1(a) minimum fields already populated as data-class stubs.\" Let me count: A(1) templated(2) code-generation(3) tool(4) that(5) stamps(6) every(7) new(8) high-risk(9) system(10) prototype(11) with(12) pre-wired(13) event-recording(14) hooks(15), structured(16) log(17) emitters(18), and(19) the(20) Annex(21) III(22) 1(a)(23) minimum(24) fields(25) already(26) populated(27) as(28) data-class(29) stubs(30). = ~30 words\n- how: \"The platform team extracts our existing experiment-tracking decorators into a shared Python and TypeScript package; CI lints every new repo to confirm the scaffold is present before merge, so engineers never hand-roll logging after the model works.\" Count: The(1) platform(2) team(3) extracts(4) our(5) existing(6) experiment-tracking(7) decorators(8) into(9) a(10) shared(11) Python(12) and(13) TypeScript(14) package(15); CI(16) lints(17) every(18) new(19) repo(20) to(21) confirm(22) the(23) scaffold(24) is(25) present(26) before(27) merge(28), so(29) engineers(30) never(31) hand-roll(32) logging(33) after(34) the(35) model(36) works(37). = ~37 words\n- why: \"Cuts the integration lead-time for compliance logging from days to zero at prototype inception, preserving our iteration velocity without deferring a har