        "content": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"As a development engineer whose KPIs are prototyping speed and technical viability, my read of Article 12 is that it inserts a mandatory runtime instrumentation layer into every high-risk system we ship, and if I integrate logging manually per project my iteration cycles slow down measurably. The good news is the prescriptive fields in Article 12(3) are finite and enumerable—four data categories—which means one well-designed middleware can satisfy them across our portfolio without bespoke work per project. My concern with Recital 71's 'lifetime' documentation duty is less about changing what we build and more about ensuring storage and query architecture survives far beyond the development sprint, which forces technical choices I make during prototyping: append-only event logs versus mutable state, retention policies, query indexing for post-market retrieval. If we get the shared infrastructure right in Q1, the marginal cost per new prototype drops to a configuration step rather than an engineering task. So this is a pipeline and toolchain adjustment, not an agenda change: same target systems, faster compliance-integrated builds.\",\n  \"stance\": 48,\n  \"proposals\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Shared Logging Middleware in Prototype Scaffolding\",\n      \"what\": \"A reusable logging SDK that auto-captures Article 12(3) minimum fields—session timestamps, reference database identifier, matched input data, and verifier identity—into a standard event stream for every high-risk prototype we build. It slots into our existing scaffolding so no project reinvents schema design from scratch.\",\n      \"how\": \"Two engineers spend four weeks building the SDK atop our current telemetry bus, then pair with two pilot projects to confirm per-event integration latency stays under five milliseconds before it becomes mandatory across all new prototypes.\",\n      \"why\": \"Converts a per-project compliance tax into a one-time infrastructure investment, directly protecting my iteration velocity and every team's prototyping speed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Compliance Logging Gates in Our CI Pipeline\",\n      \"what\": \"Automated build-time checks that fail any merge if the deployed artefact lacks Article 12(1) logging hooks or its Annex III field schema is incomplete, shifting verification from post-deployment audit to pre-commit enforcement.\",\n      \"how\": \"We extend our CI with a static-analysis step scanning for the middleware's annotation decorators plus an integration test that fires synthetic events and asserts all four Article 12(3) fields appear in output within one hundred milliseconds.\",\n      \"why\": \"Catches missing instrumentation while it costs minutes to fix rather than hours during a pre-market scrutiny review, keeping sprint cycles predictable.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Logging Overhead Benchmark for Performance-Critical Prototypes\",\n      \"what\": \"A benchmarking effort measuring CPU, memory, and p99-latency cost of full Article 12 logging on our most performance-sensitive prototypes, establishing a quantified overhead budget before architects lock in production designs.\",\n      \"how\": \"We run the shared middleware across three representative workloads at production-scale data volumes for two sprints, recording latency deltas and storage growth rates, then publish an internal note with per-system logging budgets that design reviews must respect.\",\n      \"why\": \"Gives me concrete numbers to confirm technical viability—logging meets Article 12 without breaching the client's SLA—which is the question I face most often in pre-sales architecture discussions.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
