rded event is structured to serve both Article 12 compliance and downstream scientific analysis of model behaviour, drift, and failure modes. The schema treats each log entry as an experimental record with full provenance rather than a bare audit trail.\",\n      \"how\": \"Work jointly with engineering to define a unified event schema—timestamped inputs, reference states, outputs, confidence scores—that feeds simultaneously into the tamper-evident compliance archive and a research-queryable data lake shared by all project groups in the lab.\",\n      \"why\": \"Eliminates redundant per-project instrumentation and converts a regulatory cost into shared scientific infrastructure that strengthens reproducibility across every paper our group publishes.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Mandated Logs as a Meta-Dataset on AI Reliability\",\n      \"what\": \"Exploit the longitudinal event records that Article 12(1)–(2) forces us to generate over system lifetimes as a unique corpus for studying how high-risk AI systems degrade, drift, or fail in production. This turns compliance data into a first-order research dataset.\",\n      \"how\": \"Establish a dedicated analytical workstream within the lab that treats accumulated operational logs across all deployed systems as an empirical corpus; develop statistical and machine-learning methods to extract failure taxonomies, drift signatures, and risk precursors from them.\",\n      \"why\": \"Creates genuinely novel scientific contributions on AI reliability that no non-regulated institution could replicate, positioning our lab at the knowledge frontier with a data moat built by regulation itself.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Open Technical Documentation as Field-Level Knowledge Product\",\n      \"what\": \"Recast the technical documentation mandated by Recital 71—algorithms, data provenance, training and validation processes—into structured, publishable knowledge artifacts that advance the broader community's under