nd intellectual merit, this regulation doesn't fundamentally change what questions we ask or what's scientifically interesting. It changes the methodology and infrastructure requirements. The logging is engineering overhead that we need to absorb, but it also creates new research questions (how do you design observability for AI systems? how do you make logs scientifically useful rather than just compliance artifacts?). I'd say 42 - moderate adjustment, not existential.\n\nNow 3 distinct proposals:\n\n1. Something about turning logging requirements into a research methodology improvement - designing \"scientifically-informative\" log architectures that serve both compliance AND research reproducibility/analysis. This is different from just \"build a logging system.\"\n\n2. Something about the knowledge frontier aspect - investigating how systematic event-recording (mandated by Article 12) can itself become a source of new scientific insights, e.g., drift detection, failure mode taxonomy, understanding AI system behavior over time. The logs aren't just compliance artifacts; they're datasets for meta-research on AI systems.\n\n3. Something about documentation as intellectual contribution - Recital 71 requires documenting algorithms, data, training processes in clear comprehensive form. For a research institution, this could be turned into an open-science contribution: creating a standardized technical documentation framework that advances the field's understanding of how complex AI systems work, making our internal compliance documentation a public knowledge product (where IP allows).\n\nLet me make sure these are genuinely different:\n- Proposal 1: Methodological/engineering - designing log architectures that serve dual purpose (compliance + scientific reproducibility)\n- Proposal 2: Research question generation - using the mandated logs as data for new science about AI system behavior\n- Proposal 3: Knowledge dissemination - turning documentation requirements into o